var tafsirData_26 = {
  "1": "\"Ṭâ-Sĩn-Mĩm\" opens the chapter of the poets with three disconnected letters, and the verse is one of the Quran's letter-openings, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the three letters: \"Ṭâ-Sĩn-Mĩm\", and the commentators explain the meaning with the care that the matter deserves: the disconnected letters at the openings of the chapters are among the matters whose full meaning God has kept with Himself, and the early scholars offered their readings, and the readings are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the opening, and the teaching is that the letters are the Quran's own announcement of its nature, and the most famous of the readings is the one the scholars have taught for centuries: the Book that the disbelievers call the human fabrication is made of these very letters, the letters of their own language, and the challenge stands in the letters themselves, and the standing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the challenge, and the teaching is that the letters they know cannot produce the Book they deny, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the Book, and the Book is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the opening, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the humility, and the teaching is that the reader meets the letters with the awareness of the limit, and the awareness is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's first of its kind, and the first is the chapter's teaching on the opening, and the teaching is that the letters are the Book's signature, and the signature is the chapter's answer to the accusation of the fabrication, and the answer is the challenge that stands in the letters, and the challenge is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the clear Book, and the Book is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "2": "\"These are the verses of the clear Book\" follows the letters, and the verse is the teaching on the clarity, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the claim: \"These are the verses of the clear Book\", and the word \"clear\" is 'mubin,' the word of the evident, and the verse says the verses that follow are the verses of the evident Book, and the commentators explain the meaning: the clarity is the Book's own witness, and the witness is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the Book does not hide its meanings, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the grief, and the grief is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the clarity, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the confidence, and the teaching is that the clear Book needs no defender, and the defender is the chapter's word, and the word opens the chapter's stories, and the stories are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the confidence, and the confidence is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the clear Book needs no defender, and the defender is the chapter's answer to the grief of the next verse, and the answer is that the clarity is the comfort, and the comfort is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the grief, and the grief is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the confidence, and the confidence is the clarity, and the clarity is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the clear Book needs no defender, and the defending is the chapter's answer to the accusation, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "3": "\"Perhaps you ˹O Prophet˺ will grieve yourself to death over their disbelief\" opens the chapter's comfort, and the verse is the teaching on the grief, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the fear: \"Perhaps you ˹O Prophet˺ will grieve yourself to death over their disbelief\", and the word \"grieve\" is 'bakhi'un,' the word of the destroying, and the verse says the Prophet's grief over the denial was near to destroying him, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Prophet's care for his people was so great that their denial wounded him, and the verse records the wound, and the recording is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the care, and the teaching is that the Prophet's grief was the measure of his love, and the verse then leaves the comfort to the next verses, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the power, and the power is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the grief, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the messenger, and the teaching is that the messenger's heart carries the people, and the carrying is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the care, and the care is the chapter's teaching on the messenger, and the teaching is that the Prophet's grief over the denial was near to destroying him, and the destroying is the chapter's answer to the one who imagines the messenger is the cold deliverer, and the answer is that the messenger's heart carries the people, and the carrying is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the power, and the power is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "4": "\"If We willed, We could send down upon them a ˹compelling˺ sign from the heavens, leaving their necks bent in ˹utter˺ submission to it\" states the power, and the verse is the teaching on the unforced, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the possibility: \"If We willed, We could send down upon them a ˹compelling˺ sign from the heavens, leaving their necks bent in ˹utter˺ submission to it\", and the word \"compelling\" is 'ayah,' and the verse says the sign that forces the submission was within the power, and the commentators explain the meaning: the forced faith is not the faith, and the trial of the choice is the wisdom of the unforced, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the freedom, and the teaching is that the sign that bends the necks is held back for the sake of the choice, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reminder, and the reminder is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the power, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the withheld sign is the mercy that keeps the door of the willing open, and the door is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the withheld, and the withheld is the chapter's teaching on the freedom, and the teaching is that the sign that bends the necks is held back for the sake of the choice, and the choice is the chapter's answer to the one who asks why the forced faith is not sent, and the answer is the mercy, and the mercy is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reminder, and the reminder is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "5": "\"Whatever new reminder comes to them from the Most Compassionate, they always turn away from it\" states the response, and the verse is the teaching on the turning, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the response: \"Whatever new reminder comes to them from the Most Compassionate, they always turn away from it\", and the word \"whatever\" is the verse's width, and the verse says every reminder is met with the turning, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denial is the habit, and the habit is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the denial, and the teaching is that the denier's response does not change with the reminder, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the consequence, and the consequence is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the turning, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the teaching is that the turning away is the denial's face, and the face is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the habit, and the habit is the chapter's teaching on the denial, and the teaching is that the denier's response does not change with the reminder, and the unchanging is the chapter's answer to the one who hopes the new reminder will turn the old heart, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the news, and the news is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the habit, and the habit is the denial, and the denial is the chapter's teaching on the response, and the teaching is that the denier's response does not change with the reminder, and the unchanging is the chapter's answer to the one who hopes the new word will turn the old heart, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "6": "\"They have certainly denied ˹the truth˺, so they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule\" states the consequence, and the verse is the teaching on the news, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two facts: \"They have certainly denied ˹the truth˺, so they will soon face the consequences of their ridicule\", and the word \"consequences\" is 'anba',' the word of the news, and the verse says the deniers will receive the news of their mockery, and the commentators explain the meaning: the news is the punishment, and the word \"soon\" is the verse's seal, and the saying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the mockery's news arrives, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the earth, and the earth is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the news, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is that the ridicule is recorded and the record is the news, and the news is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the news, and the news is the chapter's teaching on the record, and the teaching is that the mockery is recorded and the record is the news, and the recording is the chapter's answer to the one who mocks and forgets, and the answer is the soon, and the soon is the chapter's word, and the word is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the earth, and the earth is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "7": "\"Have they failed to look at the earth, ˹to see˺ how many types of fine plants We have caused to grow in it?\" turns to the sign, and the verse is the teaching on the earth, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Have they failed to look at the earth, ˹to see˺ how many types of fine plants We have caused to grow in it?\" and the word \"types\" is 'zawj,' and the verse says the earth grows the pairs of the fine plants, and the commentators explain the meaning: the variety of the plants is the sign, and the sign is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the earth, and the teaching is that the earth's growth is the standing proof, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the sign, and the sign is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the earth, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the looking, and the teaching is that the failed look is the denial's beginning, and the beginning is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the looking, and the looking is the chapter's teaching on the earth, and the teaching is that the earth's variety of the fine plants is the standing proof, and the proof is the chapter's answer to the one who asks for the sign while the field grows, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the sign, and the sign is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the looking, and the looking is the earth, and the earth is the chapter's teaching on the proof, and the teaching is that the earth's variety of the fine plants is the standing proof, and the standing is the chapter's answer to the one who asks for the sign while the field grows, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "8": "\"Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe\" states the sign and the response, and the verse is the teaching on the two, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two: \"Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe\", and the word \"sign\" is 'ayah,' and the verse says the earth's growth is the sign, and the verse then says the most do not believe, and the commentators explain the meaning: the sign is the clear and the belief is the rare, and the pairing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the two, and the teaching is that the sign's clarity and the heart's refusal stand side by side, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the two names, and the two names are the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the sign, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the rarity, and the teaching is that the believing is the few, and the few are the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the two, and the two is the chapter's teaching on the rarity, and the teaching is that the sign's clarity and the heart's refusal stand side by side, and the standing is the chapter's answer to the one who asks why the signs do not convert, and the answer is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the two names, and the two names are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the two, and the two is the rarity, and the rarity is the chapter's teaching on the sign, and the teaching is that the sign's clarity and the heart's refusal stand side by side, and the standing is the chapter's answer to the one who asks why the signs do not convert, and the answer is the chapter's word.",
  "9": "\"And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful\" closes the opening, and the verse is the teaching on the two names, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two names: \"And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful\", and the word \"Almighty\" is 'aziz,' and the word \"Merciful\" is 'rahim,' and the verse pairs the two, and the commentators explain the meaning: the pairing is the chapter's refrain, and the refrain is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the two names, and the teaching is that the might and the mercy are the two faces of the Lord, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the story of Moses, and the story is the chapter's next teaching, and the verse is the chapter's word on the two names, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the Almighty who could force the faith is the Merciful who leaves the door open, and the door is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the balance, and the balance is the chapter's teaching on the two names, and the teaching is that the Almighty who could force the faith is the Merciful who leaves the door open, and the opening is the chapter's answer to the fear of the power, and the answer is the chapter's refrain, and the refrain will run through the stories, and the stories are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the balance, and the balance is the two names, and the two names are the chapter's teaching on the Lord, and the teaching is that the Almighty who could force the faith is the Merciful who leaves the door open, and the opening is the chapter's answer to the fear of the power, and the answer is the chapter's refrain, and the refrain is the chapter's word.",
  "10": "“˹Remember˺ when your Lord called out to Moses, “Go to the wrongdoing people—”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"wa ith nada rabbuka Moosa ani i'ti al-qawma az-zalimeen,\" and the commentators note the call, for the Lord called Moses to the wrongdoing people, and the verse is the record of the call, the opening of the account that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the mission, and the verse is the record of the call, the opening of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the mission and the people are the wrongdoers, and the verse is the record of the call, the opening that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own duty. The believer is taught that the call of the Lord is the mission, and the one who is called is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose duty the Lord accepts, and the call to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the mission is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose duty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mission of the Lord: Moses was called and sent, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the mission is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the duty that the mission requires.",
  "11": "“the people of Pharaoh. Will they not fear ˹Allah˺?”” completes the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the call: or \"qawma Fir'awna a-la yattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord identified the people of Pharaoh and asked the fear, and the completion is the mission, the declaration of the recipient and the appeal, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mission that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the appeal is the question, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mission of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the mission and the appeal is the question, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mission that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mission, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own duty. The believer is taught that the mission of the Lord is the call to the fear, and the one who is sent is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose duty the Lord accepts, and the completion of the call is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose duty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the mission: Moses was sent to call the fear, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the duty that the mission requires.",
  "12": "“He replied, “My Lord! I fear that they will reject me.”” records the first fear, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the fear: or \"qala rabbi innee akhafu an yukaththiboonee,\" and the commentators note the fear, for Moses feared the rejection, and the verse is the record of the fear, the first of the two fears, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear is the human, and the verse is the record of the fear, the first of the plea. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fear is the human and the rejection is the fear, and the verse is the record of the fear, the first of the two fears.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humanity. The believer is taught that the fear of the rejection is the human, and the one who fears is the one whose fear the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the fear is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the fear of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the human, and the believer who reads the fear is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the fear: Moses feared and was reassured, and the believer who fears is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the fear is the teaching that the trust is the remedy. The verse is the record of the fear, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "13": "“And ˹so˺ my heart will be broken and my tongue will be tied. So send Aaron along ˹as a messenger˺.”” records the second fear, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the fear: or \"wa yadeequ sadree wa la yantaliqu lisanee fa-arsil ila Haroon,\" and the commentators note the fear, for Moses feared the constriction and the tongue, and the verse is the record of the fear, the second of the two, and the commentators note the significance, for the constriction is the fear and the tongue is the impediment, and the verse is the record of the fear, the request of the helper. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fear is the constriction and the request is the helper, and the verse is the record of the fear, the second of the two fears.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reliance. The believer is taught that the fear of the weakness is the human, and the one who asks the help is the one whose help the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the fear of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the help is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the fear is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the weak: Moses asked the helper, and the believer who asks the help is the believer whose help the Lord grants, and the fear is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the fear, and the teaching of the reliance that the weakness requires.",
  "14": "“Also, they have a charge against me, so I fear they may kill me.”” records the third fear, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the fear: or \"wa lahum 'alayya thanbun fa-akhafu an yaqtuloonee,\" and the commentators note the fear, for Moses feared the charge and the killing, and the verse is the record of the fear, the third of the plea, and the commentators note the significance, for the charge is the killing of the Egyptian, and the fear is the human, and the verse is the record of the fear, the third of the plea. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the charge is the killing and the fear is the human, and the verse is the record of the fear, the third of the plea.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the fear of the past is the human, and the one who fears is the one whose fear the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the fear of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the Lord removes, and the believer who reads the fear is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the fearful: Moses feared the charge and was reassured, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the fear is the teaching that the trust is the remedy. The verse is the record of the fear, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "15": "“Allah responded, “Certainly not! So go, both of you, with Our signs. We will be with you, listening.”” records the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the reassurance. The verse records the reassurance: or \"qala kalla fathhaba bi-ayatina inna ma'akum mustami'oon,\" and the commentators note the reassurance, for the Lord calmed the fears and commanded the going, and the reassurance is the comfort, the declaration of the presence and the listening, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the presence is the protection, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reassurance is the comfort and the presence is the protection, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who fears is the one whom the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reassurance to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the presence is the safety, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the presence of the Lord: Moses was reassured and sent, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose fear the Lord removes, and the reassurance is the teaching that the presence is the protection. The verse is the record of the reassurance, and the teaching of the trust that the presence produces.",
  "16": "“Go to Pharaoh and say, ‘We are messengers from the Lord of all worlds,’” records the message, and the commentators note the two parts of the message. The verse records the message: or \"fa'tiya Fir'awna fa-qoola inna rasoolu rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the message, for the Lord dictated the declaration, and the message is the mission, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the message, the declaration that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration of the Lord of all worlds is the argument, and the verse is the record of the message, the declaration of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the message is the declaration and the office is the mission, and the verse is the record of the message, the declaration that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the message, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own calling. The believer is taught that the declaration of the truth is the mission, and the one who declares is the one whose mission the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the Lord is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the message to Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the message is the believer whose calling the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the declaration of the mission: Moses was given the declaration, and the believer who calls to the truth is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the message is the teaching that the declaration is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the message, and the teaching of the calling that the mission requires.",
  "17": "“˹commanded to say:˺ ‘Let the Children of Israel go with us.’”” completes the message, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the message: or \"an arsil ma'ana Banee Isra'eel,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the demand is the release, and the completion is the mission, the command of the liberation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the demand that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the release is the demand, and the verse is the record of the completion, the demand of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the demand and the release is the command, and the verse is the record of the completion, the demand that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the demand, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the demand of the liberation is the justice, and the one who demands the release is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who opposes the oppression is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the demand of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the liberation is the thing that the believer must demand, and the believer who reads the demand is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the demand of the justice: Moses demanded the release, and the believer who opposes the oppression is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the demand is the teaching that the liberation is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the justice that the demand requires.",
  "18": "“Pharaoh protested, “Did we not raise you among us as a child, and you stayed several years of your life in our care?”” records the protest, and the commentators note the two parts of the protest. The verse records the protest: or \"qala alam nurabbika feena waleedan wa labithta feena min 'umurika sineen,\" and the commentators note the protest, for Pharaoh reminded Moses of the upbringing, and the protest is the reproach, the claim of the favour, and the verse is the record of the protest, the reproach that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the upbringing is the claim, and the verse is the record of the protest, the reproach of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the protest is the reproach and the upbringing is the claim, and the verse is the record of the protest, the reproach that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the protest, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the reproach of the falsehood is the claim of the favour, and the one who is reproached is the one whose answer the Lord guides, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord values, and the protest of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the reproach is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the protest is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the reproach: Moses was reproached and answered, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord guides, and the protest is the teaching that the reproach is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the protest, and the teaching of the certainty that the answer requires.",
  "19": "“Then you did what you did, being ˹utterly˺ ungrateful!”” completes the protest, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the protest: or \"wa fa'alta fa'lataka allatee fa'alta wa anta mina al-kafireen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Pharaoh accused the killing and the ingratitude, and the completion is the reproach, the charge of the past, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reproach that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the charge is the killing, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reproach of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the reproach and the charge is the killing, and the verse is the record of the completion, the reproach that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the charge of the falsehood is the reproach, and the one who is charged is the one whose answer the Lord guides, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord values, and the completion of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the reproach is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the charge: Moses was charged and answered, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord guides, and the completion is the teaching that the reproach is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the answer requires.",
  "20": "“Moses replied, “I did it then, lacking guidance.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala fa'altuha ithan wa ana mina ad-dalleen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Moses acknowledged the deed and the lack of the guidance, and the reply is the honesty, the confession of the past, and the verse is the record of the reply, the honesty that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the honesty, and the verse is the record of the reply, the honesty of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the honesty and the confession is the past, and the verse is the record of the reply, the honesty that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the honesty, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the confession of the past is the honesty, and the one who confesses is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who owns the deed is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the confession: Moses confessed the past, and the believer who owns the deed is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the honesty is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the honesty that the confession requires.",
  "21": "“So I fled from you when I feared you. Then my Lord granted me wisdom and made me one of the messengers.”” continues the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the reply: or \"fa-farartu minkum lamma khiftukum fa-wahaba lee rabbee hukman wa ja'alanee mina al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Moses declared the flight and the grant, and the continuation is the reply, the statement of the favour, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the reply that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the flight is the past and the grant is the favour, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the reply of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the reply and the grant is the favour, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the reply that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the flight of the believer is the trust, and the one who flees for the faith is the one whom the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the continuation of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the Lord rewards, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the trust: Moses fled and was granted, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord rewards, and the continuation is the teaching that the trust is the path of the grant. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the trust that the reply requires.",
  "22": "“How can that be a ‘favour,’ of which you remind me, when ˹it was only because˺ you ˹have˺ enslaved the Children of Israel?”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: or \"wa tilka ni'matun tamunnuha 'alayya an 'abbadta Banee Isra'eel,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Moses refuted the claim of the favour, and the completion is the argument, the declaration that the enslavement cancels the favour, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the enslavement is the wrong, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the enslavement is the wrong, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own discernment. The believer is taught that the refutation of the false is the argument, and the one who reasons is the one whose discernment the Lord values, and the believer who exposes the wrong is the believer whose discernment the Lord accepts, and the completion of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the argument is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Moses refuted the claim, and the believer who exposes the wrong is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the discernment that the argument requires.",
  "23": "“Pharaoh asked, “And what is ‘the Lord of all worlds’?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: or \"qala Fir'awnu wa ma rabbu al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the question, for Pharaoh asked of the Lord, and the question is the confrontation, the opening of the debate, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the test, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the dialogue. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the test and the answer is the truth, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the debate.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own answer. The believer is taught that the question of the falsehood is the occasion of the truth, and the one who is asked is the one whose answer the Lord guides, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord values, and the question of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the question is the door of the answer, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose answer the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the occasion of the question: Pharaoh asked and Moses answered, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord guides, and the question is the teaching that the inquiry is the door of the truth. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the answer that the question invites.",
  "24": "“Moses replied, “˹He is˺ the Lord of the heavens and the earth and everything in between, if only you had sure faith.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: or \"qala rabbu as-samawati wal-ardi wa ma baynahuma in kuntum mooqineen,\" and the commentators note the answer, for Moses declared the Lord of the heavens and the earth, and the answer is the argument, the declaration of the creation, and the verse is the record of the answer, the argument that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the argument, and the verse is the record of the answer, the argument of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the argument and the declaration is the creation, and the verse is the record of the answer, the argument that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the declaration of the creation is the argument, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who knows the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the answer of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the evidence, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the creation: Moses declared the Lord of the heavens and the earth, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the answer is the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "25": "“Pharaoh said to those around him, “Did you hear ˹what he said˺?”” records the mockery, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the mockery: or \"qala li-man hawlahu a-la tastami'oon,\" and the commentators note the mockery, for Pharaoh turned to the court, and the mockery is the dismissal, the attempt to belittle the answer, and the verse is the record of the mockery, the dismissal that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the mockery is the dismissal, and the verse is the record of the mockery, the dismissal of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mockery is the dismissal and the court is the audience, and the verse is the record of the mockery, the dismissal that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mockery, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the mockery of the falsehood is the dismissal, and the one who is mocked is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the mockery of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the dismissal is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the mockery is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the truth: Moses was mocked and answered, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the mockery is the teaching that the dismissal is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the mockery, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "26": "“Moses added, “˹He is˺ your Lord and the Lord of your forefathers.”” records the addition, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the addition: or \"qala rabbukum wa rabbu aba'ikumu al-awwaleen,\" and the commentators note the addition, for Moses declared the Lord of them and their fathers, and the addition is the argument, the extension of the declaration, and the verse is the record of the addition, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the extension is the argument, and the verse is the record of the addition, the argument of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the addition is the argument and the extension is the declaration, and the verse is the record of the addition, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the addition, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the extension of the declaration is the argument, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the addition of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the addition is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the persistence of the truth: Moses extended the declaration, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the addition is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the addition, and the teaching of the certainty that the declaration produces.",
  "27": "“Pharaoh said ˹mockingly˺, “Your messenger, who has been sent to you, must be insane.”” records the accusation, and the commentators note the two parts of the accusation. The verse records the accusation: or \"qala inna rasoolakumu allathee ursila ilaykum la-majnoon,\" and the commentators note the accusation, for Pharaoh declared Moses insane, and the accusation is the label, the charge of the madness, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the accusation is the label and the madness is the charge, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the label, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the madness is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the accusation of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the accusation is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Moses was labelled and answered, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the accusation is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the accusation, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "28": "“Moses responded: “˹He is˺ the Lord of the east and west, and everything in between, if only you had any sense.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala rabbu al-mashriqi wal-maghribi wa ma baynahuma in kuntum ta'qiloon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Moses declared the Lord of the east and the west, and the reply is the argument, the extension of the proof, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration is the argument, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the argument and the declaration is the creation, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the declaration of the creation is the argument, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the evidence, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the creation: Moses declared the Lord of the east and the west, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "29": "“Pharaoh threatened, “If you take any other god besides me, I will certainly have you imprisoned.”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: or \"qala la-ini ittakhathta ilahan ghayree la-aj'alannaka mina al-masjooneen,\" and the commentators note the threat, for Pharaoh threatened the prison, and the threat is the violence, the answer of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat is the violence, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the threat is the violence and the prison is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own courage. The believer is taught that the threat of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose courage the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose courage the Lord accepts, and the threat of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose courage the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the courage of the truth: Moses was threatened and answered, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose courage the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the courage that the truth requires.",
  "30": "“Moses responded, “Even if I bring you a clear proof?”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reply: or \"qala a-wa law ji'tuka bi-shay'in mubeen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Moses asked whether the proof would change the threat, and the reply is the challenge, the offer of the proof, and the verse is the record of the reply, the challenge that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the offer is the challenge, and the verse is the record of the reply, the challenge of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the challenge and the proof is the offer, and the verse is the record of the reply, the challenge that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the challenge, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the offer of the proof is the challenge, and the one who offers is the one whose confidence the Lord values, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord accepts, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the proof is the thing that the truth offers, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the challenge of the truth: Moses offered the proof, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the proof is the thing that the truth offers. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the confidence that the challenge requires.",
  "31": "“Pharaoh demanded, “Bring it then, if what you say is true.”” records the demand, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the demand: or \"qala fa'ti bihi in kunta mina as-sadiqeen,\" and the commentators note the demand, for Pharaoh demanded the proof, and the demand is the challenge, the dare of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the demand, the challenge that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the demand is the dare, and the verse is the record of the demand, the challenge of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the demand is the dare and the proof is the challenge, and the verse is the record of the demand, the challenge that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the demand, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the demand of the falsehood is the challenge, and the one who is dared is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the dare is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the demand of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the dare is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the demand is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the truth: Moses was dared and displayed, and the believer who faces the dare is the believer whose truth the Lord displays, and the demand is the teaching that the dare is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the demand, and the teaching of the confidence that the challenge requires.",
  "32": "“So he threw down his staff and—behold!—it became a real snake.”” records the miracle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the miracle: or \"fa-alqa 'asahu fa-itha hiya thu'banun mubeen,\" and the commentators note the miracle, for the staff became the snake, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the first of the signs, and the commentators note the significance, for the transformation is the proof, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the sign that the following verse continues. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the miracle is the transformation and the snake is the proof, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the sign that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the miracle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the miracle of the Lord is the proof, and the one who sees the sign is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the miracle of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the miracle is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the staff became the snake, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the miracle is the teaching that the transformation is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the miracle, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "33": "“Then he drew his hand ˹out of his collar˺ and it was ˹shining˺ white for all to see.”” records the second sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"wa naza'a yadahu fa-itha hiya bayda'u lin-nazireen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the hand shone white, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second of the signs, and the commentators note the significance, for the white hand is the light, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second sign. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the light and the white is the purity, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second of the signs.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the proofs, and the one who receives the signs is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the sign of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the hand shone white, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the sign is the teaching that the light is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "34": "“Pharaoh said to the chiefs around him, “He is indeed a skilled magician,”” records the accusation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the accusation: or \"qala lil-mala'i hawlahu inna hatha la-sahirun 'aleem,\" and the commentators note the accusation, for Pharaoh called Moses the magician, and the accusation is the label, the charge of the sorcery, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the accusation is the label and the magician is the charge, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the label, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the magic is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the accusation of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the accusation is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Moses was labelled and prevailed, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the accusation is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the accusation, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "35": "“who seeks to drive you from your land by his magic. So what do you propose?”” completes the accusation, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the accusation: or \"yureedu an yukhrijakum min ardikum bi-sihrihi fa-matha ta'muroon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Pharaoh accused the plot and asked the counsel, and the completion is the accusation, the charge of the conspiracy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the accusation that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the charge is the plot, and the verse is the record of the completion, the accusation of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the accusation and the plot is the charge, and the verse is the record of the completion, the accusation that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the accusation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the accusation of the plot is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is accused is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the completion of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the accusation is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the accusation: Moses was accused and prevailed, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the accusation is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the patience that the accusation requires.",
  "36": "“They replied, “Let him and his brother wait and dispatch mobilizers to all cities,”” records the counsel, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the counsel: or \"qaloo arjih wa akhahu wab'ath fi al-mada'ini hashireen,\" and the commentators note the counsel, for the chiefs advised the delay and the gathering, and the counsel is the plan, the preparation of the magicians, and the verse is the record of the counsel, the plan that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the counsel is the plan, and the verse is the record of the counsel, the preparation of the falsehood. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the counsel is the plan and the gathering is the preparation, and the verse is the record of the counsel, the plan that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the counsel, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the plan of the falsehood is the thing that the truth awaits, and the one who watches the scheme is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the counsel of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the plan is the prelude of the fall, and the believer who reads the counsel is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prelude of the fall: the chiefs planned and lost, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the counsel is the teaching that the plan of the falsehood is the prelude of its fall. The verse is the record of the counsel, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "37": "“to bring you every skilled magician.”” completes the counsel, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the counsel: or \"ya'tooka bi-kulli sahharin 'aleem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the purpose is the gathering of the magicians, and the completion is the plan, the summoning of the experts, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plan that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the gathering is the preparation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the summoning of the falsehood. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the plan and the gathering is the summoning, and the verse is the record of the completion, the plan that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the gathering of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who watches is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the completion of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the gathering is the prelude of the fall, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prelude of the fall: the magicians were gathered and lost, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the gathering is the prelude of the fall. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "38": "“So the magicians were assembled at the set time on the appointed day.”” records the assembly, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the assembly: or \"fa-jumi'a as-saharatu li-meeqati yawmin ma'loom,\" and the commentators note the assembly, for the magicians were gathered, and the verse is the record of the assembly, the gathering that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the assembly is the preparation, and the verse is the record of the assembly, the gathering of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the assembly is the gathering and the day is the appointment, and the verse is the record of the assembly, the gathering that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the assembly, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the assembly of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who watches is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the assembly of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the gathering is the prelude of the fall, and the believer who reads the assembly is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prelude of the fall: the magicians were assembled and lost, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the assembly is the teaching that the gathering is the prelude of the fall. The verse is the record of the assembly, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "39": "“And the people were asked, “Will you join the gathering,”” records the call, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"wa qeela lin-nasi hal antum mujtami'oon,\" and the commentators note the call, for the people were called to the gathering, and the call is the invitation, the summoning of the crowd, and the verse is the record of the call, the invitation that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the invitation, and the verse is the record of the call, the summoning of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the invitation and the gathering is the crowd, and the verse is the record of the call, the invitation that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the gathering of the crowd is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who watches is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the call to the people is the teaching, the declaration that the crowd is the arena of the truth, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the arena of the truth: the crowd was gathered and the truth prevailed, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the call is the teaching that the crowd is the arena of the truth. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the confidence that the public requires.",
  "40": "“so that we may follow the magicians if they prevail?”” completes the call, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the call: or \"la'allana nattabi'u as-saharata in kanoo humu al-ghalibeen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the hope is the following of the magicians, and the completion is the intention, the purpose of the gathering, and the verse is the record of the completion, the intention that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the hope is the intention, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose of the crowd. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the intention and the hope is the following, and the verse is the record of the completion, the intention that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the intention, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the intention of the falsehood is the following, and the one who watches is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the completion of the call is the teaching, the declaration that the intention is the prelude of the fall, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prelude of the fall: the crowd hoped the magicians and saw the truth, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the intention is the prelude of the fall. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "41": "“When the magicians came, they asked Pharaoh, “Shall we have a ˹suitable˺ reward if we prevail?”” records the request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: or \"fa-lamma ja'a as-saharatu qaloo li-Fir'awna a-inna lana la-ajran in kunna nahnu al-ghalibeen,\" and the commentators note the request, for the magicians asked the reward, and the request is the mercenary, the question of the fee, and the verse is the record of the request, the question that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the request is the mercenary, and the verse is the record of the request, the question of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the mercenary and the reward is the fee, and the verse is the record of the request, the question that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the mercenary is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is hired is the one whose sincerity the Lord weighs, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the request of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the fee is the thing that the falsehood offers, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sincerity of the service: the magicians asked the fee and found the truth, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the request is the teaching that the fee is the thing that the falsehood offers. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the sincerity that the truth requires.",
  "42": "“He replied, “Yes, and you will then certainly be among those closest to me.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: or \"qala na'am wa innakum ithan la-mina al-muqarrabeen,\" and the commentators note the answer, for Pharaoh promised the reward and the nearness, and the answer is the bribe, the offer of the court, and the verse is the record of the answer, the bribe that the following verse opens the contest with, and the commentators note the significance, for the nearness is the bribe, and the verse is the record of the answer, the offer of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the bribe and the nearness is the offer, and the verse is the record of the answer, the bribe that the following verse opens the contest with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the bribe, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the bribe of the falsehood is the offer, and the one who is bribed is the one whose sincerity the Lord weighs, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the answer of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the nearness is the thing that the falsehood offers, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sincerity of the service: the magicians were bribed and found the truth, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the answer is the teaching that the nearness is the thing that the falsehood offers. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the sincerity that the truth requires.",
  "43": "“Moses said to them, “Cast whatever you wish to cast.”” records the invitation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the invitation: or \"qala lahum Moosa alqoo ma antum mulqoon,\" and the commentators note the invitation, for Moses invited the magicians to cast, and the invitation is the confidence, the command of the truth, and the verse is the record of the invitation, the confidence that the following verse displays, and the commentators note the significance, for the invitation is the confidence, and the verse is the record of the invitation, the confidence of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the invitation is the confidence and the casting is the command, and the verse is the record of the invitation, the confidence that the following verse displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the invitation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the invitation of the truth is the confidence, and the one who invites is the one whose confidence the Lord values, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord accepts, and the invitation of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the truth does not fear the display, and the believer who reads the invitation is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the truth: Moses invited the casting, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the invitation is the teaching that the truth lets the falsehood show its full. The verse is the record of the invitation, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "44": "“So they cast down their ropes and staffs, saying, “By Pharaoh’s might, it is we who will surely prevail.”” records the display, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the display: or \"fa-alqaw hibalahum wa 'isiyyahum wa qaloo bi-'izzati Fir'awna inna la-nahnu al-ghaliboon,\" and the commentators note the display, for the magicians cast and swore by Pharaoh, and the display is the magic, the casting of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the display, the casting that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the oath by Pharaoh is the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the display, the casting of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the display is the casting and the oath is the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the display, the casting that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the display, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the display of the falsehood is the thing that the truth awaits, and the one who watches is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the display of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the display is the prelude of the fall, and the believer who reads the display is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prelude of the fall: the magicians cast and lost, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the display is the teaching that the casting is the prelude of the fall. The verse is the record of the display, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "45": "“Then Moses threw down his staff, and—behold!—it devoured the objects of their illusion!”” records the miracle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the miracle: or \"fa-alqa Moosa 'asahu fa-itha hiya talqafu ma ya'fikoon,\" and the commentators note the miracle, for the staff devoured the illusions, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the judgment that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the devouring is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the judgment of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the miracle is the devouring and the judgment is the truth, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the judgment that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the miracle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the truth devours the falsehood, and the one who trusts the power is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the miracle of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood is the thing that the truth devours, and the believer who reads the miracle is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the truth: the staff devoured the illusions, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the miracle is the teaching that the falsehood is the thing that the truth devours. The verse is the record of the miracle, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "46": "“So the magicians fell down, prostrating.”” records the prostration, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the prostration: or \"fa-ulqiya as-saharatu sajideen,\" and the commentators note the prostration, for the magicians fell, and the prostration is the conversion, the surrender of the experts, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the conversion that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the prostration is the conversion, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the conversion of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prostration is the conversion and the falling is the surrender, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the conversion that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prostration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the truth converts the sincere, and the one who sees the proof is the one whose conversion the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the prostration of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the experts confess, and the believer who reads the prostration is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the truth: the magicians prostrated, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the prostration is the teaching that the truth converts the sincere. The verse is the record of the prostration, and the teaching of the certainty that the truth produces.",
  "47": "“They declared, “We ˹now˺ believe in the Lord of all worlds—”” records the confession, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the confession: or \"qaloo amanna bi-rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the confession, for the magicians declared the faith, and the confession is the conversion, the declaration of the belief, and the verse is the record of the confession, the declaration that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the conversion, and the verse is the record of the confession, the declaration of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the confession is the conversion and the declaration is the faith, and the verse is the record of the confession, the declaration that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the confession, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the confession of the truth is the conversion, and the one who declares is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the confession of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must declare, and the believer who reads the confession is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confession of the truth: the magicians declared and held, and the believer who declares the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the confession is the teaching that the declaration is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the confession, and the teaching of the faith that the declaration establishes.",
  "48": "“the Lord of Moses and Aaron.”” completes the confession, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the confession: or \"rabbi Moosa wa Haroon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the magicians named the two, and the completion is the confession, the specification of the faith, and the verse is the record of the completion, the confession that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the naming is the specification, and the verse is the record of the completion, the confession of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the confession and the naming is the specification, and the verse is the record of the completion, the confession that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the specification of the faith is the confession, and the one who names is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the completion of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the thing that the believer must specify, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the specification of the faith: the magicians named the two, and the believer who specifies the faith is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the specification is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the faith that the specification establishes.",
  "49": "“Pharaoh threatened, “How dare you believe in him before I give you permission? He must be your master who taught you magic, but soon you will see. I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, then crucify you all.”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: or \"qala amantum lahu qabla an athana lakum innahu la-kabeerukumu allathee 'allamakumu as-sihra fa-la-sawfa ta'lamoona la-uqatti'anna aydiyakum wa arjulakum min khilafin wa la-usallibannakum ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the threat, for Pharaoh threatened the amputation and the crucifixion, and the threat is the violence, the answer of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat is the violence, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the threat is the violence and the amputation is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the threat of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose faith the Lord tests, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the threat of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the faith withstands, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose steadfastness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trial of the threat: the magicians were threatened and held, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the violence is the test. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the steadfastness that the threat demands.",
  "50": "“They responded, “˹That would be˺ no harm! Surely to our Lord we will ˹all˺ return.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo la dayra inna ila rabbina munqaliboon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the magicians declared the return, and the reply is the faith, the calm of the converted, and the verse is the record of the reply, the faith that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the calm is the faith, and the verse is the record of the reply, the faith of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the faith and the return is the certainty, and the verse is the record of the reply, the faith that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the faith, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the faith is the calm, and the one who knows the return is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord confirms, and the reply of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the return is the thing that the believer knows, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the calm of the faith: the magicians held and returned, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the return is the thing that the believer knows. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the faith that the certainty produces.",
  "51": "“We really hope that our Lord will forgive our sins, as we are the first to believe.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: or \"inna natma'u an yaghfira lana rabbuna khatayana an kunna awwala al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the magicians hoped the forgiveness, and the completion is the faith, the hope of the converted, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope that the following verse addresses, and the commentators note the significance, for the hope is the faith, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the hope and the forgiveness is the request, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope that the following verse addresses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the hope of the forgiveness is the faith, and the one who repents is the one whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the completion of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hope of the repentance: the magicians hoped and were accepted, and the believer who repents is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "52": "“And We inspired Moses, ˹saying,˺ “Leave with My servants at night, for you will surely be pursued.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"wa awhayna ila Moosa an asri bi-'ibadee innakum muttaba'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the night departure, and the command is the guidance, the instruction of the exodus, and the verse is the record of the command, the guidance that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the night is the departure, and the verse is the record of the command, the guidance of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the guidance and the night is the departure, and the verse is the record of the command, the guidance that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the command of the Lord is the guidance, and the one who obeys is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the command to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the path of the safety, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the guidance of the exodus: Moses obeyed and was saved, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the command is the teaching that the trust is the safety. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the trust that the guidance produces.",
  "53": "“Then Pharaoh sent mobilizers to all cities,” records the pursuit, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the pursuit: or \"fa-arsala Fir'awnu fi al-mada'ini hashireen,\" and the commentators note the pursuit, for Pharaoh sent the mobilizers, and the pursuit is the aggression, the gathering of the host, and the verse is the record of the pursuit, the aggression that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the pursuit is the aggression, and the verse is the record of the pursuit, the gathering of the army. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pursuit is the aggression and the mobilizers are the gatherers, and the verse is the record of the pursuit, the aggression that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pursuit, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the pursuit of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who is pursued is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the pursuit of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the aggression is the thing that the truth withstands, and the believer who reads the pursuit is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the aggression of the falsehood: Pharaoh pursued and drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the pursuit is the teaching that the aggression is the thing that the truth withstands. The verse is the record of the pursuit, and the teaching of the trust that the aggression requires.",
  "54": "“˹and said,˺ “These ˹outcasts˺ are just a handful of people,”” records the taunt, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the taunt: or \"inna ha'ula'i la-shirthimatun qaleeloon,\" and the commentators note the taunt, for Pharaoh belittled the people, and the taunt is the arrogance, the contempt of the few, and the verse is the record of the taunt, the arrogance that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the taunt is the arrogance, and the verse is the record of the taunt, the contempt of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the taunt is the arrogance and the few is the contempt, and the verse is the record of the taunt, the arrogance that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the taunt, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the taunt of the falsehood is the arrogance, and the one who is belittled is the one whose honour the Lord values, and the believer who bears the taunt is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the taunt of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the taunt is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the arrogance of the falsehood: Pharaoh taunted and drowned, and the believer who bears the taunt is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the taunt is the teaching that the arrogance is the thing that the falsehood holds. The verse is the record of the taunt, and the teaching of the humility that the taunt requires.",
  "55": "“who have really enraged us,”” continues the taunt, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the taunt: or \"wa innahum lana la-gha'izoon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Pharaoh declared the rage, and the continuation is the taunt, the confession of the anger, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the taunt that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rage is the confession, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the taunt of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the taunt and the rage is the confession, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the taunt that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the rage of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who is enraged is the one whose rage the Lord judges, and the believer who bears the rage is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the continuation of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the rage is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the rage of the falsehood: Pharaoh raged and drowned, and the believer who bears the rage is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the rage is the thing that the falsehood holds. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the patience that the rage requires.",
  "56": "“but we are all on the alert.”” completes the taunt, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the taunt: or \"wa inna la-jamee'un hathiroon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Pharaoh declared the alertness, and the completion is the taunt, the claim of the readiness, and the verse is the record of the completion, the taunt that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the alertness is the claim, and the verse is the record of the completion, the taunt of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the taunt and the alertness is the claim, and the verse is the record of the completion, the taunt that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the claim of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who claims is the one whose claim the Lord nullifies, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the claim is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the nullification of the claim: Pharaoh claimed and drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the claim is the thing that the falsehood holds. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the claim requires.",
  "57": "“So We lured the tyrants out of ˹their˺ gardens, springs,” records the luring, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the luring: or \"fa-akhrajnahum min jannatin wa 'uyoon,\" and the commentators note the luring, for the Lord lured the tyrants out, and the luring is the judgment, the removal of the blessings, and the verse is the record of the luring, the judgment that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the luring is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the luring, the removal of the blessings. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the luring is the judgment and the removal is the loss, and the verse is the record of the luring, the judgment that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the luring, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the removal of the blessings is the judgment, and the one who is lured is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the luring of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the luring is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the tyrants were lured and drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the luring is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the luring, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "58": "“treasures, and splendid residences.”” continues the luring, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the luring: or \"wa kunoozin wa maqamin kareem,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for the Lord listed the treasures and the residences, and the continuation is the luring, the enumeration of the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the luring that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the enumeration is the luring, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the removal of the blessings. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the luring and the enumeration is the loss, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the luring that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the removal of the blessings is the judgment, and the one who is lured is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the continuation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the tyrants lost the blessings, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the continuation is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "59": "“So it was. And We awarded it ˹all˺ to the Children of Israel.”” records the inheritance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the inheritance: or \"kathalika wa awrathnaha Banee Isra'eel,\" and the commentators note the inheritance, for the Lord awarded the blessings to the Children of Israel, and the inheritance is the reward, the grant of the land, and the verse is the record of the inheritance, the reward that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the inheritance is the reward, and the verse is the record of the inheritance, the grant of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the inheritance is the reward and the grant is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the inheritance, the reward that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inheritance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the inheritance of the Lord is the reward, and the one who is granted is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the inheritance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the inheritance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the Lord: the blessings were granted to the believers, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the inheritance is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the inheritance, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "60": "“And so they pursued them at sunrise.”” records the pursuit, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the pursuit: or \"fa-atba'oohum mushriqeen,\" and the commentators note the pursuit, for the tyrants pursued at the sunrise, and the pursuit is the aggression, the chase of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the pursuit, the aggression that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the pursuit is the aggression, and the verse is the record of the pursuit, the chase of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pursuit is the aggression and the sunrise is the time, and the verse is the record of the pursuit, the aggression that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the pursuit, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the pursuit of the falsehood is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who is pursued is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the pursuit of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the aggression is the thing that the truth withstands, and the believer who reads the pursuit is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the aggression of the falsehood: Pharaoh pursued and drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the pursuit is the teaching that the aggression is the thing that the truth withstands. The verse is the record of the pursuit, and the teaching of the trust that the aggression requires.",
  "61": "“When the two groups came face to face, the companions of Moses cried out, “We are overtaken for sure.”” records the cry, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the cry: or \"fa-lamma tara'a al-jam'ani qala ashabu Moosa inna la-mudrakoon,\" and the commentators note the cry, for the companions cried the overtaking, and the cry is the fear, the panic of the people, and the verse is the record of the cry, the fear that the following verse calms, and the commentators note the significance, for the cry is the fear, and the verse is the record of the cry, the panic of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the cry is the fear and the overtaking is the panic, and the verse is the record of the cry, the fear that the following verse calms.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the cry, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the fear of the trial is the human, and the one who cries is the one whose fear the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the cry of the companions is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the Lord removes, and the believer who reads the cry is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the fear: the companions cried and were reassured, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the cry is the teaching that the trust is the remedy of the fear. The verse is the record of the cry, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "62": "“Moses reassured ˹them˺, “Absolutely not! My Lord is certainly with me—He will guide me.”” records the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the reassurance. The verse records the reassurance: or \"qala kalla inna ma'iya rabbee sa-yahdeen,\" and the commentators note the reassurance, for Moses declared the presence and the guidance, and the reassurance is the trust, the calm of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the trust that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the presence is the protection, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the trust of Moses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reassurance is the trust and the presence is the protection, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the trust that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the presence of the Lord is the protection, and the one who trusts the presence is the one whose fear the Lord removes, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reassurance of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the presence is the safety, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the presence of the Lord: Moses trusted and was guided, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose fear the Lord removes, and the reassurance is the teaching that the presence is the protection. The verse is the record of the reassurance, and the teaching of the trust that the presence produces.",
  "63": "“So We inspired Moses: “Strike the sea with your staff,” and the sea was split, each part was like a huge mountain.”” records the miracle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the miracle: or \"fa-awhayna ila Moosa ani idrib bi-'asaka al-bahra fanfalaqa fa-kana kullu firqin kat-tawdi al-'azeem,\" and the commentators note the miracle, for the Lord commanded the striking and the sea split, and the miracle is the salvation, the parting of the sea, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the salvation that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the splitting is the salvation, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the parting of the sea. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the miracle is the splitting and the salvation is the parting, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the salvation that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the miracle, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose salvation the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the trust is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the miracle of the sea is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord displays, and the believer who reads the miracle is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the sea split and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the miracle is the teaching that the power is the thing that the Lord displays. The verse is the record of the miracle, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "64": "“We drew the pursuers to that place,” records the drawing, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the drawing: or \"wa azlafna thamma al-akhareen,\" and the commentators note the drawing, for the Lord drew the pursuers, and the drawing is the judgment, the bringing of the tyrants, and the verse is the record of the drawing, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the drawing is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drawing, the bringing of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the drawing is the judgment and the bringing is the end, and the verse is the record of the drawing, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the drawing, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose judgment the Lord brings, and the believer who holds the trust is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drawing of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the drawing is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the tyrants were drawn and drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drawing is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the drawing, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "65": "“and delivered Moses and those with him all together.”” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the deliverance: or \"wa anjayna Moosa wa man ma'ahu ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord saved Moses and the believers, and the deliverance is the mercy, the salvation of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the deliverance is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the salvation of the believers. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the mercy and the salvation is the reward, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the salvation is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: Moses was delivered, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the deliverance is the teaching that the salvation is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "66": "“Then We drowned the others.”” records the drowning, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the drowning: or \"thumma aghraqna al-akhareen,\" and the commentators note the drowning, for the Lord drowned the others, and the drowning is the judgment, the end of the tyrants, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the end of Pharaoh. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the drowning is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the drowning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the trust is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the drowning of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the drowning is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the tyrants drowned and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drowning is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the drowning, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "67": "“Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the story is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the sign is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the lesson and the denial is the heedlessness, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the lessons, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the sign of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the sign: the story is the lesson, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the sign is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the reflection that the lesson invites.",
  "68": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "69": "“Relate to them ˹O Prophet˺ the story of Abraham,” opens the account, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse commands the narration: or \"watlu 'alayhim naba'a Ibraheem,\" and the commentators note the command, for the Prophet was commanded to relate the story of Abraham, and the command is the introduction, the opening of the account that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the story is the argument, and the verse is the record of the command, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the introduction and the story is the argument, and the verse is the record of the command, the opening that the following verses unfold.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the narration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the story of Abraham is the argument, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the story is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the command of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the story: Abraham is the model, and the believer who reads the story is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the reflection that the story invites.",
  "70": "“when he questioned his father and his people, “What is that you worship ˹besides Allah˺?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The verse records the question: or \"ith qala li-abeehi wa qawmihi ma ta'budoon,\" and the commentators note the question, for Abraham asked the worship, and the question is the argument, the opening of the debate, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the argument, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the debate. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the argument and the worship is the object, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reasoning. The believer is taught that the question of the false is the argument, and the one who asks is the one whose reasoning the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord accepts, and the question of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the argument is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose reasoning the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the question: Abraham asked and exposed, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reasoning that the argument requires.",
  "71": "“They replied, “We worship idols, to which we are fully devoted.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo na'budu asnaman fa-nazallu laha 'akifeen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people confessed the idols, and the reply is the confession, the declaration of the worship, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the honesty, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the confession and the idols are the object, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own discernment. The believer is taught that the confession of the false is the honesty, and the one who confesses is the one whose falsehood the reason exposes, and the believer who seeks the truth is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the confession is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confession of the false: the people confessed and were exposed, and the believer who seeks the truth is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the confession is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the discernment that the seeking requires.",
  "72": "“Abraham asked, “Can they hear you when you call upon them?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: or \"qala hal yasma'oonakum ith tad'oon,\" and the commentators note the question, for Abraham asked the hearing, and the question is the argument, the first of the three, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the argument, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the argument and the hearing is the first, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reasoning. The believer is taught that the question of the false is the argument, and the one who asks is the one whose reasoning the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord accepts, and the question of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the argument is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose reasoning the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the question: Abraham asked the hearing, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reasoning that the argument requires.",
  "73": "“Or can they benefit or harm you?”” completes the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the question: or \"aw yanfa'oonakum aw yadurroon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham asked the benefit and the harm, and the completion is the argument, the second and the third of the questions, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the three questions are the argument, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the three are the questions, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the argument of the false is the thing that the truth uses, and the one who reasons is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the completion of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the argument is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Abraham asked the benefit and the harm, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the argument produces.",
  "74": "“They replied, “No! But we found our forefathers doing the same.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo bal wajadna aba'ana kathalika yaf'aloon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people appealed to the forefathers, and the reply is the confession, the declaration that the ground is the inheritance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the inheritance is the weakness, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the confession and the inheritance is the ground, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own discernment. The believer is taught that the inheritance is not the proof, and the one who appeals to the fathers is the one whose appeal the reason refutes, and the believer who seeks the proof is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the inheritance is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weakness of the inheritance: the people appealed to the fathers, and the believer who seeks the proof is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the inheritance is not the evidence. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the discernment that the seeking requires.",
  "75": "“Abraham responded, “Have you ˹really˺ considered what you have been worshipping—”” records the response, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the response: or \"qala a-fa-ra'aytum ma kuntum ta'budoon,\" and the commentators note the response, for Abraham asked the consideration, and the response is the argument, the invitation to the reflection, and the verse is the record of the response, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the consideration is the argument, and the verse is the record of the response, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the response is the argument and the consideration is the invitation, and the verse is the record of the response, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the response, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the consideration is the argument, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the response of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the truth seeks, and the believer who reads the response is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the truth: Abraham asked the consideration, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the response is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the truth seeks. The verse is the record of the response, and the teaching of the reflection that the argument invites.",
  "76": "“you and your ancestors?”” completes the response, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the response: or \"antum wa aba'ukumu al-aqdamoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham included the ancestors, and the completion is the argument, the extension of the consideration, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the extension is the argument, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the extension is the inclusion, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the extension of the argument is the reflection, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the completion of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the truth seeks, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the truth: Abraham included the ancestors, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the truth seeks. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the reflection that the argument invites.",
  "77": "“They are ˹all˺ enemies to me, except the Lord of all worlds.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the declaration. The verse records the declaration: or \"fa-innahum 'aduwwun lee illa rabba al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Abraham declared the idols the enemies, and the declaration is the severing, the statement of the loyalty, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the severing that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the enmity is the severing, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the loyalty of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the severing and the enmity is the loyalty, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the severing that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own loyalty. The believer is taught that the severing of the false is the loyalty, and the one who severs is the one whose loyalty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose loyalty the Lord accepts, and the declaration of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the severing is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose loyalty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the severing of the false: Abraham declared the enmity, and the believer who severs the false is the believer whose loyalty the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the severing is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the loyalty that the severing requires.",
  "78": "“˹He is˺ the One Who created me, and He ˹alone˺ guides me.”” records the first attribute, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the attribute: or \"allathee khalaqanee fa-huwa yahdeen,\" and the commentators note the attribute, for Abraham declared the creation and the guidance, and the attribute is the argument, the first of the favours, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the creation and the guidance are the favours, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the attribute is the argument and the favours are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the attribute, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the creation and the guidance are the proofs, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who knows the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the attribute of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the evidence, and the believer who reads the attribute is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proof of the creation: Abraham declared the creation and the guidance, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the attribute is the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the attribute, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "79": "“˹He is˺ the One Who provides me with food and drink.”” records the second attribute, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the attribute: or \"wallathee huwa yut'imunee wa yasqeen,\" and the commentators note the attribute, for Abraham declared the provision, and the attribute is the argument, the second of the favours, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the provision is the favour, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the attribute is the argument and the provision is the favour, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the attribute, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the provision of the Lord is the favour, and the one who declares is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the attribute of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the provision is the favour, and the believer who reads the attribute is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the favour of the provision: Abraham declared the provision, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the attribute is the teaching that the provision is the favour. The verse is the record of the attribute, and the teaching of the gratitude that the provision requires.",
  "80": "“And He ˹alone˺ heals me when I am sick.”” records the third attribute, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the attribute: or \"wa itha maridtu fa-huwa yashfeen,\" and the commentators note the attribute, for Abraham declared the healing, and the attribute is the argument, the third of the favours, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the healing is the favour, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the attribute is the argument and the healing is the favour, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the attribute, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the healing of the Lord is the favour, and the one who declares is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the healer is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the attribute of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the healing is the favour, and the believer who reads the attribute is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the favour of the healing: Abraham declared the healing, and the believer who trusts the healer is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the attribute is the teaching that the healing is the favour. The verse is the record of the attribute, and the teaching of the trust that the healing requires.",
  "81": "“And He ˹is the One Who˺ will cause me to die, and then bring me back to life.”” records the fourth attribute, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the attribute: or \"wallathee yumeetunee thumma yuhyeen,\" and the commentators note the attribute, for Abraham declared the death and the resurrection, and the attribute is the argument, the fourth of the favours, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the death and the resurrection are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the attribute is the argument and the death and the resurrection are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the attribute, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the attribute, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the death and the resurrection are the proofs, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who knows the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the attribute of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the death and the resurrection are the evidence, and the believer who reads the attribute is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proof of the resurrection: Abraham declared the death and the resurrection, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the attribute is the teaching that the resurrection is the evidence. The verse is the record of the attribute, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "82": "“And He is ˹the One˺ Who, I hope, will forgive my flaws on Judgment Day.”” completes the attributes, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the attributes: or \"wallathee atma'u an yaghfira lee khatee'atee yawma ad-deen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham hoped the forgiveness, and the completion is the faith, the hope of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the hope is the faith, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the hope and the forgiveness is the request, and the verse is the record of the completion, the hope that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own repentance. The believer is taught that the hope of the forgiveness is the faith, and the one who hopes is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who repents is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the completion of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hope of the repentance: Abraham hoped and was answered, and the believer who repents is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the completion is the teaching that the hope is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "83": "““My Lord! Grant me wisdom, and join me with the righteous.”” records the first request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: or \"rabbi hab lee hukman wa alhiqnee bis-saliheen,\" and the commentators note the request, for Abraham asked the wisdom and the company, and the request is the prayer, the seeking of the judgment and the righteous, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the wisdom is the gift, and the company is the hope, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the prayer and the wisdom is the gift, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the wisdom and the company are the things that the believer seeks, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the righteous is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the seeking is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the seeking: Abraham asked the wisdom and the company, and the believer who prays is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the request is the teaching that the seeking is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "84": "“Bless me with honourable mention among later generations.”” records the second request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: or \"waj'al lee lisana sidqin fi al-akhireen,\" and the commentators note the request, for Abraham asked the honourable mention, and the request is the prayer, the seeking of the good name, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the mention is the good name, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the prayer and the mention is the good name, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the honourable mention is the gift, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the good name is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the good name is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the good name: Abraham asked the mention, and the believer who hopes the good name is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request is the teaching that the good name is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "85": "“Make me one of those awarded the Garden of Bliss.”” records the third request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: or \"waj'alnee min warathati jannati an-na'eem,\" and the commentators note the request, for Abraham asked the Garden, and the request is the prayer, the seeking of the Paradise, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the Garden is the hope, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the prayer and the Garden is the hope, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the Garden is the hope, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the Paradise is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the Paradise is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the Paradise: Abraham asked the Garden, and the believer who hopes the Paradise is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request is the teaching that the Paradise is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "86": "“Forgive my father, for he is certainly one of the misguided.”” records the fourth request, and the commentators note the two parts of the request. The verse records the request: or \"waghfir li-abee innahu kana mina ad-dalleen,\" and the commentators note the request, for Abraham asked the forgiveness of the father, and the request is the prayer, the intercession of the son, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the prayer and the intercession is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own mercy. The believer is taught that the prayer for the parent is the mercy, and the one who intercedes is the one whose mercy the Lord values, and the believer who prays for the family is the believer whose mercy the Lord accepts, and the request of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the prayer for the family is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose mercy the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the intercession: Abraham prayed for the father, and the believer who prays for the family is the believer whose mercy the Lord values, and the request is the teaching that the prayer for the family is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the mercy that the intercession requires.",
  "87": "“And do not disgrace me on the Day all will be resurrected—”” records the fifth request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: or \"wa la tukhzinee yawma yub'athoon,\" and the commentators note the request, for Abraham asked the sparing of the disgrace, and the request is the prayer, the seeking of the safety, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the disgrace is the fear, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the request is the prayer and the disgrace is the fear, and the verse is the record of the request, the prayer that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the sparing of the disgrace is the hope, and the one who prays is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who hopes the safety is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the safety is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the safety: Abraham asked the sparing, and the believer who hopes the safety is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the request is the teaching that the safety is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "88": "“the Day when neither wealth nor children will be of any benefit.”” describes the Day, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse describes the Day: or \"yawma la yanfa'u malun wa la banoon,\" and the commentators note the description, for the wealth and the children do not benefit, and the description is the teaching, the declaration that the Day is the reckoning, and the verse is the record of the description, the teaching that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the two are the things that the Day nullifies, and the verse is the record of the description, the teaching of the Day. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the description is the teaching and the two are the nullified, and the verse is the record of the description, the teaching that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the description, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the Day nullifies the wealth and the children, and the one who knows is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who prepares the heart is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the description of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the Day is the thing that the believer must prepare for, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the Day of the reckoning: the wealth and the children do not benefit, and the believer who prepares the heart is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the description is the teaching that the Day is the thing that the believer must prepare for. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the certainty that the Day produces.",
  "89": "“Only those who come before Allah with a pure heart ˹will be saved˺.”” completes the description, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the description: or \"illa man ata Allaha bi-qalbin saleem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the pure heart is the condition, and the completion is the teaching, the declaration that the heart is the thing that the Day weighs, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the \"qalb saleem\" is the sound heart, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching of the Day. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the teaching and the heart is the condition, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the heart, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the sound heart is the condition, and the one who purifies is the one whose heart the Lord values, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the heart is the thing that the Day weighs, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sound heart: the heart is the condition, and the believer who purifies the heart is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the heart is the thing that the Day weighs. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the sincerity that the heart requires.",
  "90": "“˹On that Day˺ Paradise will be brought near to the God-fearing,” records the nearness, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the nearness: or \"wa uzlifati al-jannatu lil-muttaqeen,\" and the commentators note the nearness, for the Paradise is brought near, and the verse is the record of the nearness, the reward that the following verse contrasts, and the commentators note the significance, for the nearness is the reward, and the verse is the record of the nearness, the reward of the God-fearing. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the nearness is the reward and the Paradise is the end, and the verse is the record of the nearness, the reward that the following verse contrasts.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the nearness, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the nearness of the Paradise is the reward, and the one who fears is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the Paradise is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the nearness of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the Paradise is the thing that the believer seeks, and the believer who reads the nearness is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the fear: the Paradise is brought near, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the nearness is the teaching that the Paradise is the thing that the believer seeks. The verse is the record of the nearness, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "91": "“and the Hellfire will be displayed to the deviant.”” records the display, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the display: or \"wa burrizati al-jaheemu lil-ghaween,\" and the commentators note the display, for the Hell is displayed, and the verse is the record of the display, the warning that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the display is the warning, and the verse is the record of the display, the warning of the deviant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the display is the warning and the Hell is the end, and the verse is the record of the display, the warning that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the display, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the display of the Hell is the warning, and the one who is deviant is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the Hell is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the display of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the Hell is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the display is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the warning of the Hell: the Hell is displayed, and the believer who fears the Hell is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the display is the teaching that the Hell is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the display, and the teaching of the fear that the warning produces.",
  "92": "“And it will be said to them, “Where are those you used to worship”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: or \"wa qeela lahum ayna ma kuntum ta'budoon,\" and the commentators note the question, for the deviant are asked of the idols, and the question is the rebuke, the exposure of the false, and the verse is the record of the question, the rebuke that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the rebuke, and the verse is the record of the question, the exposure of the idols. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the rebuke and the exposure is the false, and the verse is the record of the question, the rebuke that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the question of the Day is the rebuke, and the one who is asked is the one whose falsehood the Lord exposes, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the question of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the Day exposes, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the exposure of the false: the deviant are asked and exposed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the question is the teaching that the false is the thing that the Day exposes. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "93": "“besides Allah? Can they help you or even help themselves?”” completes the question, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the question: or \"min dooni Allahi hal yansuroonakum aw yantasiroon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the question asks the help, and the completion is the rebuke, the exposure of the powerlessness, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the help is the question, and the verse is the record of the completion, the exposure of the idols. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the rebuke and the help is the question, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the idols are the powerless, and the one who is exposed is the one whose falsehood the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the Day exposes, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the exposure of the false: the idols cannot help, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the false is the thing that the Day exposes. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "94": "“Then the idols will be hurled headlong into Hell, along with the deviant” records the hurling, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the hurling: or \"fa-kubkiboo feeha hum wal-ghawoon,\" and the commentators note the hurling, for the idols and the deviant are hurled, and the hurling is the judgment, the casting of the false, and the verse is the record of the hurling, the judgment that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the hurling is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the hurling, the casting of the idols. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hurling is the judgment and the casting is the end, and the verse is the record of the hurling, the judgment that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hurling, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the casting of the false is the judgment, and the one who is hurled is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the end is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the hurling of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the Day casts, and the believer who reads the hurling is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the false: the idols and the deviant are hurled, and the believer who fears the end is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the hurling is the teaching that the false is the thing that the Day casts. The verse is the record of the hurling, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "95": "“and the soldiers of Iblîs, all together.”” continues the hurling, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the hurling: or \"wa junoodu Ibleesa ajma'oon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for the soldiers of Iblis are hurled, and the continuation is the judgment, the casting of the host, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the judgment that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the host is the cast, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the casting of the soldiers. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the judgment and the host is the cast, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the judgment that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the casting of the host is the judgment, and the one who follows is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the end is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the continuation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the host is the thing that the Day casts, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the host: the soldiers are hurled, and the believer who fears the end is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the host is the thing that the Day casts. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "96": "“There the deviant will cry while disputing with their idols,” records the dispute, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the dispute: or \"qaloo wa hum feeha yakhtasimoon,\" and the commentators note the dispute, for the deviant dispute with the idols, and the dispute is the regret, the argument of the Hell, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the regret that the following verse records, and the commentators note the significance, for the dispute is the regret, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the argument of the deviant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the dispute is the regret and the argument is the Hell, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the regret that the following verse records.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the dispute, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the dispute of the Hell is the regret, and the one who argues is the one whose regret the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the dispute of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in, and the believer who reads the dispute is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the regret of the false: the deviant dispute and regret, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the dispute is the teaching that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in. The verse is the record of the dispute, and the teaching of the certainty that the regret produces.",
  "97": "““By Allah! We were clearly mistaken,”” records the confession, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the confession: or \"tallahi in kunna la-fee dalalin mubeen,\" and the commentators note the confession, for the deviant confess the error, and the confession is the regret, the admission of the misguidance, and the verse is the record of the confession, the regret that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the regret, and the verse is the record of the confession, the admission of the deviant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the confession is the regret and the error is the admission, and the verse is the record of the confession, the regret that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the confession, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the confession of the Hell is the regret, and the one who confesses is the one whose regret the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the confession of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in, and the believer who reads the confession is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the regret of the false: the deviant confess and regret, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the confession is the teaching that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in. The verse is the record of the confession, and the teaching of the certainty that the regret produces.",
  "98": "“when we made you equal to the Lord of all worlds.”” completes the confession, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the confession: or \"ith nusawweekum bi-rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the deviant confess the equality, and the completion is the regret, the admission of the association, and the verse is the record of the completion, the regret that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the equality is the association, and the verse is the record of the completion, the admission of the deviant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the regret and the equality is the association, and the verse is the record of the completion, the regret that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the association is the error, and the one who associates is the one whose regret the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the association is the thing that the falsehood ends in, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the regret of the association: the deviant confessed and regretted, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the association is the thing that the falsehood ends in. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the regret produces.",
  "99": "“And none led us astray other than the wicked.”” records the blame, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the blame: or \"wa ma adallana illa al-mujrimoon,\" and the commentators note the blame, for the deviant blame the wicked, and the blame is the shifting, the deflection of the fault, and the verse is the record of the blame, the shifting that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the blame is the shifting, and the verse is the record of the blame, the deflection of the deviant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the blame is the shifting and the deflection is the fault, and the verse is the record of the blame, the shifting that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the blame, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the shifting of the fault is the thing that the falsehood does, and the one who blames is the one whose blame the Lord weighs, and the believer who owns the fault is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the blame of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the shifting is the thing that the falsehood does, and the believer who reads the blame is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the shifting of the false: the deviant blamed and were judged, and the believer who owns the fault is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the blame is the teaching that the shifting is the thing that the falsehood does. The verse is the record of the blame, and the teaching of the honesty that the fault requires.",
  "100": "“Now we have none to intercede for us,”” records the loss, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the loss: or \"fa-ma lana min shafi'een,\" and the commentators note the loss, for the deviant have no intercessor, and the loss is the regret, the absence of the intercession, and the verse is the record of the loss, the regret that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the absence is the regret, and the verse is the record of the loss, the absence of the intercessor. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the loss is the regret and the absence is the end, and the verse is the record of the loss, the regret that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the loss, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the absence of the intercessor is the regret, and the one who loses is the one whose regret the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the loss of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in, and the believer who reads the loss is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the regret of the false: the deviant have no intercessor, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the loss is the teaching that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in. The verse is the record of the loss, and the teaching of the certainty that the regret produces.",
  "101": "“nor a close friend.”” continues the loss, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the loss: or \"wa la sadeeqin hameem,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for the deviant have no friend, and the continuation is the regret, the absence of the friend, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the regret that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the absence is the regret, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the absence of the friend. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the regret and the absence is the end, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the regret that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the absence of the friend is the regret, and the one who loses is the one whose regret the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the continuation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the regret of the false: the deviant have no friend, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the continuation is the teaching that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the certainty that the regret produces.",
  "102": "“If only we could have a second chance, then we would be believers.”” completes the regret, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the regret: or \"fa-law anna lana karratan fa-nakoona mina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the deviant wish the return, and the completion is the regret, the wish of the second chance, and the verse is the record of the completion, the regret that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the wish is the regret, and the verse is the record of the completion, the wish of the deviant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the regret and the wish is the return, and the verse is the record of the completion, the regret that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own urgency. The believer is taught that the wish of the second chance is the regret, and the one who wishes is the one whose regret the Lord declares, and the believer who hastens the faith is the believer whose urgency the Lord values, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose urgency the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the urgency of the faith: the deviant wish the return and regret, and the believer who hastens the faith is the believer whose urgency the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the regret is the thing that the falsehood ends in. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the urgency that the regret produces.",
  "103": "“Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the story is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the sign is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the lesson and the denial is the heedlessness, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the lessons, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the sign of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the sign: the story is the lesson, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the sign is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the reflection that the lesson invites.",
  "104": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "105": "“The people of Noah rejected the messengers” opens the story, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the rejection: or \"kaththabat qawmu Noohin al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the people of Noah denied the messengers, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection of the one is the rejection of the line, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the denial and the messengers are the line, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the rejection of the messenger is the denial of the line, and the one who rejects is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the rejection: the people rejected and were drowned, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the faith that the acceptance requires.",
  "106": "“when their brother Noah said to them, “Will you not fear ˹Allah˺?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"ith qala lahum akhoohum Noohun a-la tattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the call, for Noah called the fear, and the call is the message, the appeal of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the fear, and the verse is the record of the call, the message of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the message and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the fear is the message, and the one who fears is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the call of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the fear: Noah called and was denied, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the worship that the fear requires.",
  "107": "“I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: or \"innee lakum rasoolun ameen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Noah declared the trust, and the declaration is the message, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the message and the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the trust is the office, and the one who declares is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the declaration of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the office: Noah declared the trust, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the honesty that the office requires.",
  "108": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for Noah commanded the fear and the obedience, and the command is the message, the two demands of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the two demands are the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the message of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the message and the two demands are the call, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the fear and the obedience are the message, and the one who obeys is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Noah commanded and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "109": "“I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from the Lord of all worlds.”” records the disavowal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the disavowal: or \"wa ma as'alukum 'alayhi min ajrin in ajriya illa 'ala rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the disavowal, for Noah declared the reward, and the disavowal is the refrain, the declaration of the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the disavowal is the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the disavowal is the integrity and the reward is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the disavowal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the disavowal of the reward is the integrity, and the one who seeks the Lord is the one whose sincerity the Lord values, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the disavowal of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the disavowal is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the integrity of the messenger: Noah disavowed the reward, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the disavowal is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the disavowal, and the teaching of the sincerity that the office requires.",
  "110": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” repeats the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse repeats the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the repetition, for Noah repeated the command, and the repetition is the emphasis, the confirmation of the message, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the repetition is the emphasis, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the repetition is the emphasis and the command is the message, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repetition, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the repetition of the command is the emphasis, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the repetition of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the repetition is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Noah repeated and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the repetition is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the repetition, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "111": "“They argued, “How can we believe in you, when you are followed ˹only˺ by the lowest of the low?”” records the objection, and the commentators note the two parts of the objection. The verse records the objection: or \"qaloo a-nu'minu laka wattaba'aka al-arthaloon,\" and the commentators note the objection, for the people objected to the followers, and the objection is the rejection, the contempt of the weak, and the verse is the record of the objection, the rejection that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the contempt is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the objection, the rejection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the objection is the rejection and the contempt is the weak, and the verse is the record of the objection, the rejection that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the objection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the contempt of the weak is the rejection, and the one who is objected is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the contempt is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the objection of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the contempt is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the objection is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the contempt: Noah was objected and answered, and the believer who bears the contempt is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the objection is the teaching that the contempt is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the objection, and the teaching of the patience that the contempt requires.",
  "112": "“He responded, “And what knowledge do I have of what they do?”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala wa ma 'ilmee bima kanoo ya'maloon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Noah declined the judgment, and the reply is the humility, the refusal to judge the hidden, and the verse is the record of the reply, the humility that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the refusal is the humility, and the verse is the record of the reply, the humility of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the humility and the refusal is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the reply, the humility that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the refusal to judge the hidden is the humility, and the one who declines is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who leaves the judgment to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the reply of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the judgment: Noah declined and left the judgment, and the believer who leaves the judgment to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the humility that the judgment requires.",
  "113": "“Their judgment is with my Lord, if you had any sense!”” continues the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the reply: or \"in hisabuhum illa 'ala rabbee law tash'uroon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Noah attributed the judgment to the Lord, and the continuation is the humility, the declaration that the reckoning is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the humility that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the attribution is the humility, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the humility of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the humility and the attribution is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the humility that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the attribution of the judgment is the humility, and the one who attributes is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who leaves the reckoning to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the continuation of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the attribution of the judgment: Noah attributed and left, and the believer who leaves the reckoning to the Lord is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the humility that the judgment requires.",
  "114": "“I am not going to expel the believers.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the reply: or \"wa ma ana bi-taridi al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Noah refused the expulsion, and the completion is the loyalty, the defence of the believers, and the verse is the record of the completion, the loyalty that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the refusal is the loyalty, and the verse is the record of the completion, the loyalty of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the loyalty and the refusal is the defence, and the verse is the record of the completion, the loyalty that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own loyalty. The believer is taught that the defence of the believers is the loyalty, and the one who refuses the expulsion is the one whose loyalty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the dignity is the believer whose loyalty the Lord accepts, and the completion of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the loyalty is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose loyalty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the loyalty of the defence: Noah refused the expulsion, and the believer who defends the weak is the believer whose loyalty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the loyalty is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the loyalty that the defence requires.",
  "115": "“I am only sent with a clear warning.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the reply: or \"in ana illa natheerun mubeen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Noah declared the warning, and the completion is the humility, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the completion, the humility that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the office, and the verse is the record of the completion, the humility of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the humility and the warning is the office, and the verse is the record of the completion, the humility that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the declaration of the office is the humility, and the one who declares is the one whose humility the Lord values, and the believer who knows the limit is the believer whose humility the Lord accepts, and the completion of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humility of the office: Noah declared the warning, and the believer who knows the limit is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the humility is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the humility that the office requires.",
  "116": "“They threatened, “If you do not desist, O Noah, you will surely be stoned ˹to death˺.”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: or \"qaloo la-in lam tantahi ya Noohu la-takoonanna mina al-marjoomeen,\" and the commentators note the threat, for the people threatened the stoning, and the threat is the violence, the answer of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat is the violence, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the threat is the violence and the stoning is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the threat of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the threat of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the threat: Noah was threatened and prayed, and the believer who bears the threat is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the patience that the threat requires.",
  "117": "“Noah prayed, “My Lord! My people have truly rejected me.”” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The verse records the prayer: or \"qala rabbi inna qawmee kaththaboonee,\" and the commentators note the prayer, for Noah sought the Lord, and the prayer is the recourse, the turning of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the recourse and the rejection is the cause, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reliance. The believer is taught that the prayer of the denied is the recourse, and the one who seeks the Lord is the one whose help the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the prayer of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the recourse of the denied: Noah prayed and was answered, and the believer who seeks the Lord is the believer whose help the Lord grants, and the prayer is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the reliance that the denial requires.",
  "118": "“So judge between me and them decisively, and save me and the believers with me.”” completes the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the prayer: or \"faftah baynee wa baynahum fathan wa najjinee wa man ma'iya mina al-mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Noah asked the judgment and the salvation, and the completion is the prayer, the request of the decision, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the judgment is the request, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the prayer and the judgment is the request, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reliance. The believer is taught that the prayer of the judgment is the recourse, and the one who seeks the decision is the one whose decision the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the completion of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the judgment: Noah asked the decision and was answered, and the believer who seeks the Lord is the believer whose decision the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the reliance that the judgment requires.",
  "119": "“So We saved him and those with him in the fully loaded Ark.”” records the salvation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the salvation: or \"fa-anjaynahu wa man ma'ahu fi al-fulki al-mashhoon,\" and the commentators note the salvation, for the Lord saved Noah and the believers, and the salvation is the mercy, the rescue of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the salvation, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the salvation is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the salvation, the rescue of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the salvation is the mercy and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the salvation, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the salvation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the salvation of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the salvation of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the salvation is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: Noah was saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the salvation is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the salvation, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "120": "“Then afterwards We drowned the rest.”” records the drowning, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the drowning: or \"thumma aghraqna ba'du al-baqeen,\" and the commentators note the drowning, for the Lord drowned the rest, and the drowning is the judgment, the end of the deniers, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the end of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the drowning is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the drowning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the drowning of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the drowning is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the deniers drowned and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drowning is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the drowning, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "121": "“Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the story is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the sign is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the lesson and the denial is the heedlessness, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the lessons, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the sign of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the sign: the story is the lesson, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the sign is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the reflection that the lesson invites.",
  "122": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "123": "“The people of ’Âd rejected the messengers” opens the story, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the rejection: or \"kaththabat 'Adun al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the 'Ad denied the messengers, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection is the denial, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the denial and the messengers are the line, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the rejection of the messenger is the denial, and the one who rejects is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the rejection: the 'Ad rejected and were destroyed, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the faith that the acceptance requires.",
  "124": "“when their brother Hûd said to them, “Will you not fear ˹Allah˺?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"ith qala lahum akhoohum Hoodun a-la tattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the call, for Hud called the fear, and the call is the message, the appeal of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the fear, and the verse is the record of the call, the message of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the message and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the fear is the message, and the one who fears is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the call of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the fear: Hud called and was denied, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the worship that the fear requires.",
  "125": "“I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: or \"innee lakum rasoolun ameen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Hud declared the trust, and the declaration is the message, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the message and the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the trust is the office, and the one who declares is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the declaration of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the office: Hud declared the trust, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the honesty that the office requires.",
  "126": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for Hud commanded the fear and the obedience, and the command is the message, the two demands of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the two demands are the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the message of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the message and the two demands are the call, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the fear and the obedience are the message, and the one who obeys is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Hud commanded and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "127": "“I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from the Lord of all worlds.”” records the disavowal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the disavowal: or \"wa ma as'alukum 'alayhi min ajrin in ajriya illa 'ala rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the disavowal, for Hud declared the reward, and the disavowal is the refrain, the declaration of the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the disavowal is the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the disavowal is the integrity and the reward is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the disavowal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the disavowal of the reward is the integrity, and the one who seeks the Lord is the one whose sincerity the Lord values, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the disavowal of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the disavowal is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the integrity of the messenger: Hud disavowed the reward, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the disavowal is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the disavowal, and the teaching of the sincerity that the office requires.",
  "128": "“˹Why˺ do you build a landmark on every high place in vanity,” records the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the rebuke: or \"a-tabnoonoona bi-kulli ree'in ayatan ta'bathoon,\" and the commentators note the rebuke, for Hud rebuked the landmarks, and the rebuke is the correction, the exposure of the vanity, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the landmark is the vanity, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rebuke is the correction and the vanity is the sin, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the vanity is the sin, and the one who is rebuked is the one whose vanity the Lord weighs, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the rebuke of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the vanity is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the rebuke is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the vanity: Hud rebuked the landmarks, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the rebuke is the teaching that the vanity is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the rebuke, and the teaching of the humility that the vanity requires.",
  "129": "“and construct castles, as if you are going to live forever,” continues the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the rebuke: or \"wa tattakhithoona masani'a la'allakum takhludoon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Hud rebuked the castles, and the continuation is the correction, the exposure of the permanence, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the correction that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the castle is the illusion, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the correction of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the correction and the permanence is the illusion, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the correction that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own humility. The believer is taught that the illusion of the permanence is the sin, and the one who builds for the ages is the one whose illusion the Lord weighs, and the believer who knows the end is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the continuation of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the illusion is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the illusion of the permanence: Hud rebuked the castles, and the believer who knows the end is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the illusion is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the humility that the illusion requires.",
  "130": "“and act so viciously when you attack ˹others˺?” completes the rebuke, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the rebuke: or \"wa itha batashtum batashtum jabbareen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Hud rebuked the viciousness, and the completion is the correction, the exposure of the tyranny, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the viciousness is the tyranny, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the correction and the viciousness is the tyranny, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the viciousness is the tyranny, and the one who is rebuked is the one whose tyranny the Lord weighs, and the believer who is just is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the completion of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the tyranny is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the tyranny: Hud rebuked the viciousness, and the believer who is just is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the tyranny is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the justice that the tyranny requires.",
  "131": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” repeats the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse repeats the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the repetition, for Hud repeated the command, and the repetition is the emphasis, the confirmation of the message, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the repetition is the emphasis, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the repetition is the emphasis and the command is the message, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repetition, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the repetition of the command is the emphasis, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the repetition of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the repetition is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Hud repeated and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the repetition is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the repetition, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "132": "“Fear the One Who has provided you with ˹the good˺ things you know:” records the appeal, and the commentators note the two parts of the appeal. The verse records the appeal: or \"wattaqoo allathee amaddakum bima ta'lamoon,\" and the commentators note the appeal, for Hud called the fear of the Provider, and the appeal is the argument, the reminder of the favour, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the provision is the favour, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the argument of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the appeal is the argument and the provision is the favour, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the appeal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the reminder of the favour is the argument, and the one who recalls is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the appeal of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the provision is the thing that the believer must recall, and the believer who reads the appeal is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the favour: Hud recalled the provision, and the believer who thanks the favour is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the appeal is the teaching that the provision is the thing that the believer must recall. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favour requires.",
  "133": "“He provided you with cattle, and children,” continues the appeal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the appeal: or \"amaddakum bi-an'amin wa baneen,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Hud recalled the cattle and the children, and the continuation is the argument, the enumeration of the favours, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the two are the favours, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the argument and the two are the favours, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the enumeration of the favours is the argument, and the one who recalls is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the continuation of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the favours are the things that the believer must recall, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the favours: Hud recalled the cattle and the children, and the believer who thanks the favours is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the favours are the things that the believer must recall. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favours require.",
  "134": "“and gardens, and springs.”” completes the appeal, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the appeal: or \"wa jannatin wa 'uyoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Hud recalled the gardens and the springs, and the completion is the argument, the enumeration of the favours, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the two are the favours, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the two are the favours, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own gratitude. The believer is taught that the enumeration of the favours is the argument, and the one who recalls is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the completion of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the favours are the things that the believer must recall, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the favours: Hud recalled the gardens and the springs, and the believer who thanks the favours is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the favours are the things that the believer must recall. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favours require.",
  "135": "“I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous day.”” completes the appeal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the appeal: or \"innee akhafu 'alaykum 'athaba yawmin 'azeem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Hud warned the torment, and the completion is the warning, the fear of the love, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the fear, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning of Hud. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the warning and the fear is the love, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the warning of the torment is the fear, and the one who warns is the one whose fear the Lord values, and the believer who warns the loved is the believer whose fear the Lord accepts, and the completion of Hud is the teaching, the declaration that the warning is the thing that the believer must deliver, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the warning of the torment: Hud warned and was denied, and the believer who warns the loved is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the warning is the thing that the believer must deliver. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the fear that the warning requires.",
  "136": "“They responded, “It is all the same to us whether you warn ˹us˺ or not.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo sawa'un 'alayna a-wa'azta am lam takun mina al-wa'izeen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people declared the indifference, and the reply is the rejection, the dismissal of the warning, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the indifference is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the rejection and the indifference is the dismissal, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the indifference of the falsehood is the rejection, and the one who is dismissed is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the indifference is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the indifference is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the indifference: Hud was dismissed and destroyed, and the believer who bears the indifference is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the indifference is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the indifference requires.",
  "137": "“This is simply the tradition of our predecessors.”” records the appeal, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the appeal: or \"in hatha illa khuluqu al-awwaleen,\" and the commentators note the appeal, for the people appealed to the tradition, and the appeal is the rejection, the claim of the inheritance, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the rejection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the tradition is the claim, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the rejection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the appeal is the rejection and the tradition is the claim, and the verse is the record of the appeal, the rejection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the appeal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own discernment. The believer is taught that the appeal to the tradition is the rejection, and the one who appeals is the one whose appeal the reason refutes, and the believer who seeks the truth is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the appeal of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the tradition is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the appeal is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weakness of the tradition: the people appealed and were destroyed, and the believer who seeks the truth is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the appeal is the teaching that the tradition is not the proof. The verse is the record of the appeal, and the teaching of the discernment that the seeking requires.",
  "138": "“And we will never be punished.”” completes the appeal, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the appeal: or \"wa ma nahnu bi-mu'aththabeen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the people denied the punishment, and the completion is the rejection, the claim of the safety, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the denial is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the rejection and the denial is the claim, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the denial of the punishment is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the one who denies is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the denial: the people denied and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the denial cannot shake.",
  "139": "“So they rejected him, and ˹so˺ We destroyed them. Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the result, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the result: or \"fa-kaththaboohu fa-ahlaknahum inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the result, for the Lord destroyed the deniers, and the result is the judgment, the end of the 'Ad, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the destruction is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the result is the judgment and the destruction is the end, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the result, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the destruction of the deniers is the judgment, and the one who rejects is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the result of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rejection is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the result is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the rejection: the 'Ad rejected and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the result is the teaching that the rejection is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the result, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "140": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "141": "“The people of Thamûd rejected the messengers” opens the story, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the rejection: or \"kaththabat Thamoodu al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the Thamud denied the messengers, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection is the denial, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the denial and the messengers are the line, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the rejection of the messenger is the denial, and the one who rejects is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the rejection: the Thamud rejected and were destroyed, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the faith that the acceptance requires.",
  "142": "“when their brother Ṣâliḥ said to them, “Will you not fear ˹Allah˺?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"ith qala lahum akhoohum Salihun a-la tattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the call, for Salih called the fear, and the call is the message, the appeal of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the fear, and the verse is the record of the call, the message of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the message and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the fear is the message, and the one who fears is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the call of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the fear: Salih called and was denied, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the worship that the fear requires.",
  "143": "“I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: or \"innee lakum rasoolun ameen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Salih declared the trust, and the declaration is the message, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the message and the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the trust is the office, and the one who declares is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the declaration of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the office: Salih declared the trust, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the honesty that the office requires.",
  "144": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for Salih commanded the fear and the obedience, and the command is the message, the two demands of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the two demands are the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the message of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the message and the two demands are the call, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the fear and the obedience are the message, and the one who obeys is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Salih commanded and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "145": "“I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from the Lord of all worlds.”” records the disavowal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the disavowal: or \"wa ma as'alukum 'alayhi min ajrin in ajriya illa 'ala rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the disavowal, for Salih declared the reward, and the disavowal is the refrain, the declaration of the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the disavowal is the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the disavowal is the integrity and the reward is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the disavowal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the disavowal of the reward is the integrity, and the one who seeks the Lord is the one whose sincerity the Lord values, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the disavowal of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the disavowal is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the integrity of the messenger: Salih disavowed the reward, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the disavowal is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the disavowal, and the teaching of the sincerity that the office requires.",
  "146": "“Do you think you will be ˹forever˺ left secure in what you have here:” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: or \"a-tutrakoona fee ma hahuna amineen,\" and the commentators note the question, for Salih asked the security, and the question is the argument, the exposure of the illusion, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the security is the illusion, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the argument and the security is the illusion, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the security of the world is the illusion, and the one who is asked is the one whose illusion the Lord exposes, and the believer who knows the end is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the question of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the security is the thing that the world cannot give, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the illusion of the security: Salih asked and exposed, and the believer who knows the end is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the security is the illusion. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the certainty that the exposure produces.",
  "147": "“amid gardens and springs,” continues the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the question: or \"fee jannatin wa 'uyoon,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Salih recalled the gardens, and the continuation is the argument, the enumeration of the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the gardens are the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the argument and the gardens are the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the enumeration of the blessings is the argument, and the one who reflects is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who reads the favours is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the continuation of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the blessings are the things that the believer must reflect upon, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the blessings: Salih recalled the gardens, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the blessings are the things that the believer must reflect upon. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the certainty that the reflection produces.",
  "148": "“and ˹various˺ crops, and palm trees ˹loaded˺ with tender fruit;” continues the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the question: or \"wa zuroo'in wa nakhlin tal'uha hadeem,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Salih recalled the crops and the palms, and the continuation is the argument, the enumeration of the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the two are the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the argument and the two are the blessings, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the enumeration of the blessings is the argument, and the one who reflects is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who reads the favours is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the continuation of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the blessings are the things that the believer must reflect upon, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the blessings: Salih recalled the crops and the palms, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the blessings are the things that the believer must reflect upon. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the certainty that the reflection produces.",
  "149": "“to carve homes in the mountains with great skill?” completes the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the question: or \"wa tanhitoona mina al-jibali buyootan fariheen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Salih recalled the carving, and the completion is the argument, the enumeration of the skill, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the carving is the skill, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the carving is the skill, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the skill of the world is the blessing, and the one who reflects is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who reads the favours is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the completion of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the blessings are the things that the believer must reflect upon, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the blessings: Salih recalled the carving, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the blessings are the things that the believer must reflect upon. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the reflection produces.",
  "150": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” repeats the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse repeats the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the repetition, for Salih repeated the command, and the repetition is the emphasis, the confirmation of the message, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the repetition is the emphasis, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the repetition is the emphasis and the command is the message, and the verse is the record of the repetition, the message that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the repetition, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the repetition of the command is the emphasis, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the repetition of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the repetition is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Salih repeated and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the repetition is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the repetition, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "151": "“And do not follow the command of the transgressors,” continues the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse continues the warning: or \"wa la tutee'oo amra al-muslifeen,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Salih warned the following of the transgressors, and the continuation is the warning, the prohibition of the obedience, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the warning that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the prohibition is the warning, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the warning of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the warning and the prohibition is the obedience, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the warning that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the following of the transgressors is the sin, and the one who is warned is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who obeys the Lord is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the continuation of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must direct, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the direction of the obedience: Salih warned the following, and the believer who obeys the Lord is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must direct. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the obedience that the warning requires.",
  "152": "“who spread corruption throughout the land, never setting things right.”” completes the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the warning: or \"allatheena yufsidoona fi al-ardi wa la yuslihoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Salih described the corruptors, and the completion is the warning, the description of the sin, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the corruption is the sin, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the warning and the corruption is the sin, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the corruption is the sin, and the one who is warned is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who sets things right is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the completion of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the corruption is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the corruption: Salih warned the corruptors, and the believer who sets things right is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the corruption is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the justice that the corruption requires.",
  "153": "“They replied, “You are simply bewitched!”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo innama anta mina al-musahhareen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people accused the bewitchment, and the reply is the label, the charge of the sorcery, and the verse is the record of the reply, the label that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the reply, the label of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the label and the bewitchment is the charge, and the verse is the record of the reply, the label that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the label, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the magic is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Salih was labelled and answered, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "154": "“You are only a human being like us, so bring forth a sign if what you say is true.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: or \"ma anta illa basharun mithluna fa'ti bi-ayatin in kunta mina as-sadiqeen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the people demanded the sign, and the completion is the challenge, the dare of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the completion, the challenge that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the challenge is the dare, and the verse is the record of the completion, the challenge of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the challenge and the sign is the dare, and the verse is the record of the completion, the challenge that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the challenge, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the challenge of the falsehood is the opportunity, and the one who is dared is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the challenge is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the completion of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the challenge is the thing that the truth answers, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the occasion of the challenge: Salih was dared and displayed, and the believer who faces the challenge is the believer whose truth the Lord displays, and the completion is the teaching that the challenge is the thing that the truth answers. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the confidence that the challenge requires.",
  "155": "“Ṣâliḥ said, “Here is a camel. She will have her turn to drink as you have yours, each on an appointed day.”” records the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"qala hathihi naqatun laha shirbun wa lakum shirbu yawmin ma'loom,\" and the commentators note the sign, for Salih brought the camel, and the sign is the miracle, the division of the water, and the verse is the record of the sign, the miracle that the following verse commands, and the commentators note the significance, for the camel is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sign, the miracle of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the camel and the division is the test, and the verse is the record of the sign, the miracle that the following verse commands.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the sign of the Lord is the miracle, and the one who receives is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the sign of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the camel was the sign, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the sign is the teaching that the miracle is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "156": "“And do not ever touch her with harm, or you will be overtaken by the torment of a tremendous day.”” records the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the warning. The verse records the warning: or \"wa la tamassooha bi-soo'in fa-ya'khuthakum 'athabu yawmin 'azeem,\" and the commentators note the warning, for Salih warned the harm, and the warning is the command, the prohibition of the harm, and the verse is the record of the warning, the command that the following verse shows the breach of, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the command, and the verse is the record of the warning, the command of Salih. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the command and the harm is the prohibition, and the verse is the record of the warning, the command that the following verse shows the breach of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the prohibition of the harm is the command, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who honours the sign is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the warning of Salih is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Salih warned and the people breached, and the believer who honours the sign is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the warning is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "157": "“But they killed her, becoming regretful.”” records the killing, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the killing: or \"fa-'aqarooha fa-asbahoo nadimeen,\" and the commentators note the killing, for the people killed the camel, and the killing is the sin, the breach of the command, and the verse is the record of the killing, the sin that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the killing is the sin, and the verse is the record of the killing, the breach of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the killing is the sin and the regret is the late, and the verse is the record of the killing, the sin that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the killing, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the breach of the command is the sin, and the one who breaches is the one whose sin the Lord weighs, and the believer who obeys is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the killing of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the breach is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the killing is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the breach: the people killed and regretted, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the killing is the teaching that the breach is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the killing, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "158": "“So the punishment overtook them. Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the punishment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the punishment: or \"fa-akhathahumu al-'athabu inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the punishment, for the Lord overtook the people, and the punishment is the judgment, the end of the Thamud, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the punishment is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the punishment is the judgment and the overtaking is the end, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the punishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the punishment of the breach is the judgment, and the one who breaches is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the punishment of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the breach is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the breach: the people were punished, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the punishment is the teaching that the breach is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the punishment, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "159": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "160": "“The people of Lot rejected the messengers” opens the story, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the rejection: or \"kaththabat qawmu Lootin al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the people of Lot denied the messengers, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection is the denial, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the denial and the messengers are the line, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the rejection of the messenger is the denial, and the one who rejects is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the rejection: the people of Lot rejected and were destroyed, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the faith that the acceptance requires.",
  "161": "“when their brother Lot said to them, “Will you not fear ˹Allah˺?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"ith qala lahum akhoohum Lootun a-la tattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the call, for Lot called the fear, and the call is the message, the appeal of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the fear, and the verse is the record of the call, the message of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the message and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the fear is the message, and the one who fears is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the call of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the fear: Lot called and was denied, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the worship that the fear requires.",
  "162": "“I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: or \"innee lakum rasoolun ameen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Lot declared the trust, and the declaration is the message, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the message and the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the trust is the office, and the one who declares is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the declaration of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the office: Lot declared the trust, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the honesty that the office requires.",
  "163": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for Lot commanded the fear and the obedience, and the command is the message, the two demands of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the two demands are the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the message of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the message and the two demands are the call, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the fear and the obedience are the message, and the one who obeys is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Lot commanded and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "164": "“I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from the Lord of all worlds.”” records the disavowal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the disavowal: or \"wa ma as'alukum 'alayhi min ajrin in ajriya illa 'ala rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the disavowal, for Lot declared the reward, and the disavowal is the refrain, the declaration of the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the disavowal is the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the disavowal is the integrity and the reward is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the disavowal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the disavowal of the reward is the integrity, and the one who seeks the Lord is the one whose sincerity the Lord values, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the disavowal of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the disavowal is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the integrity of the messenger: Lot disavowed the reward, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the disavowal is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the disavowal, and the teaching of the sincerity that the office requires.",
  "165": "“Why do you ˹men˺ lust after fellow men,” records the rebuke, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the rebuke: or \"a-ta'toona ath-thukrana mina al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the rebuke, for Lot rebuked the sin, and the rebuke is the correction, the exposure of the shameful, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rebuke is the correction, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rebuke is the correction and the sin is the shameful, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the correction that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own purity. The believer is taught that the rebuke of the sin is the correction, and the one who rebukes is the one whose purity the Lord values, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord accepts, and the rebuke of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the rebuke is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the rebuke of the sin: Lot rebuked the shameful, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the rebuke is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the rebuke, and the teaching of the purity that the chastity requires.",
  "166": "“leaving the wives that your Lord has created for you? In fact, you are a transgressing people.”” completes the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the rebuke: or \"wa tatharoona ma khalaqa lakum rabbukum min azwajikum bal antum qawmun 'adoon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Lot rebuked the abandonment, and the completion is the correction, the exposure of the transgression, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the abandonment is the transgression, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the correction and the transgression is the sin, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own purity. The believer is taught that the abandonment of the lawful is the transgression, and the one who rebukes is the one whose purity the Lord values, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord accepts, and the completion of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the correction of the transgression: Lot rebuked the abandonment, and the believer who guards the chastity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the purity that the chastity requires.",
  "167": "“They threatened, “If you do not desist, O Lot, you will surely be expelled.”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: or \"qaloo la-in lam tantahi ya Lootu la-takoonanna mina al-mukhrijeen,\" and the commentators note the threat, for the people threatened the expulsion, and the threat is the violence, the answer of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat is the violence, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the threat is the violence and the expulsion is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the threat of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the threat of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the threat: Lot was threatened and prayed, and the believer who bears the threat is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the patience that the threat requires.",
  "168": "“Lot responded, “I am truly one of those who despise your ˹shameful˺ practice.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala innee li-'amalikum mina al-qaleen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Lot declared the aversion, and the reply is the purity, the statement of the distance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the purity that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the aversion is the purity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the purity of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the purity and the aversion is the distance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the purity that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own purity. The believer is taught that the aversion of the sin is the purity, and the one who declares is the one whose purity the Lord values, and the believer who distances from the sin is the believer whose purity the Lord accepts, and the reply of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the purity of the aversion: Lot declared the distance, and the believer who distances from the sin is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the purity that the aversion requires.",
  "169": "“My Lord! Save me and my family from ˹the consequences of˺ what they do.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: or \"rabbi najjinee wa ahlee mimma ya'maloon,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Lot prayed the salvation, and the completion is the prayer, the recourse of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the recourse, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the prayer and the salvation is the request, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reliance. The believer is taught that the prayer of the salvation is the recourse, and the one who seeks is the one whose salvation the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the completion of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the salvation: Lot prayed and was saved, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the reliance that the salvation requires.",
  "170": "“So We saved him and all of his family,” records the salvation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the salvation: or \"fa-najjaynahu wa ahlahu ajma'een,\" and the commentators note the salvation, for the Lord saved Lot and the family, and the salvation is the mercy, the rescue of the faithful, and the verse is the record of the salvation, the mercy that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the salvation is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the salvation, the rescue of Lot. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the salvation is the mercy and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the salvation, the mercy that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the salvation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own hope. The believer is taught that the salvation of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is saved is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the salvation of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the salvation is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the salvation: Lot was saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the salvation is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the salvation, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "171": "“except an old woman, who was one of the doomed.”” qualifies the salvation, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse qualifies the salvation: or \"illa 'ajoozan fi al-ghabireen,\" and the commentators note the qualification, for the wife was excepted, and the qualification is the teaching, the declaration that the kinship does not save, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the exception is the teaching, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching of the faith. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the qualification is the teaching and the exception is the faith, and the verse is the record of the qualification, the teaching that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the qualification, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the kinship does not save, and the one who is excepted is the one whose faith the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the qualification of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith is the condition of the salvation, and the believer who reads the qualification is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the condition of the salvation: the wife was excepted, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose salvation the Lord grants, and the qualification is the teaching that the faith is the condition. The verse is the record of the qualification, and the teaching of the faith that the salvation requires.",
  "172": "“Then We utterly destroyed the rest,” records the destruction, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the destruction: or \"thumma dammarna al-akhareen,\" and the commentators note the destruction, for the Lord destroyed the rest, and the destruction is the judgment, the end of the deniers, and the verse is the record of the destruction, the judgment that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the destruction is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the destruction, the end of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the destruction is the judgment and the end is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the destruction, the judgment that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destruction, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the destruction of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the destruction is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the deniers were destroyed and the believers were saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the destruction is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the destruction, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "173": "“pouring upon them a rain ˹of brimstone˺. How evil was the rain of those who had been warned!”” completes the destruction, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the destruction: or \"wa amtarna 'alayhim mataran fa-sa'a mataru al-munthareen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord rained the brimstone, and the completion is the judgment, the description of the rain, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the rain is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the judgment and the rain is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own fear. The believer is taught that the rain of the brimstone is the judgment, and the one who is warned is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the Lord: the rain fell upon the warned, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the judgment is the thing that the Lord brings. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "174": "“Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the story is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the sign is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the lesson and the denial is the heedlessness, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the lessons, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the sign of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the sign: the story is the lesson, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the sign is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the reflection that the lesson invites.",
  "175": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the following verse opens the next story with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "176": "“The residents of the Forest rejected the messengers” opens the story, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the rejection: or \"kaththaba ashabu al-aykati al-mursaleen,\" and the commentators note the rejection, for the people of the Forest denied the messengers, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection is the denial, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rejection is the denial and the messengers are the line, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the opening that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own faith. The believer is taught that the rejection of the messenger is the denial, and the one who rejects is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the rejection: the people of the Forest rejected and were destroyed, and the believer who accepts the messenger is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the faith that the acceptance requires.",
  "177": "“when Shu’aib said to them, “Will you not fear ˹Allah˺?”” records the call, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the call: or \"ith qala lahum Shu'aybun a-la tattaqoon,\" and the commentators note the call, for Shuayb called the fear, and the call is the message, the appeal of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the fear, and the verse is the record of the call, the message of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the message and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the call, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the fear is the message, and the one who fears is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord accepts, and the call of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the fear: Shuayb called and was denied, and the believer who calls to the fear is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the fear is the thing that the believer must call to. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the worship that the fear requires.",
  "178": "“I am truly a trustworthy messenger to you.”” records the declaration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the declaration: or \"innee lakum rasoolun ameen,\" and the commentators note the declaration, for Shuayb declared the trust, and the declaration is the message, the statement of the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the message and the trust is the office, and the verse is the record of the declaration, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the declaration, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own honesty. The believer is taught that the trust is the office, and the one who declares is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the declaration of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the declaration is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the office: Shuayb declared the trust, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the declaration is the teaching that the trust is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the declaration, and the teaching of the honesty that the office requires.",
  "179": "“So fear Allah, and obey me.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: or \"fattaqoo Allaha wa atee'oon,\" and the commentators note the command, for Shuayb commanded the fear and the obedience, and the command is the message, the two demands of the prophet, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the two demands are the message, and the verse is the record of the command, the message of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the message and the two demands are the call, and the verse is the record of the command, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own obedience. The believer is taught that the fear and the obedience are the message, and the one who obeys is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the obedience: Shuayb commanded and was denied, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the message requires.",
  "180": "“I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺. My reward is only from the Lord of all worlds.”” records the disavowal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the disavowal: or \"wa ma as'alukum 'alayhi min ajrin in ajriya illa 'ala rabbi al-'alameen,\" and the commentators note the disavowal, for Shuayb declared the reward, and the disavowal is the refrain, the declaration of the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the disavowal is the integrity, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the disavowal is the integrity and the reward is the Lord's, and the verse is the record of the disavowal, the message that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the disavowal, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own sincerity. The believer is taught that the disavowal of the reward is the integrity, and the one who seeks the Lord is the one whose sincerity the Lord values, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the disavowal of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the disavowal is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the integrity of the messenger: Shuayb disavowed the reward, and the believer who serves for the truth is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the disavowal is the teaching that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the disavowal, and the teaching of the sincerity that the office requires.",
  "181": "“Give full measure, and cause no loss ˹to others˺.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: or \"awfoo al-kayla wa la takoonoo mina al-mukhsireen,\" and the commentators note the command, for Shuayb commanded the full measure, and the command is the correction, the demand of the justice, and the verse is the record of the command, the correction that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the measure is the justice, and the verse is the record of the command, the correction of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the correction and the measure is the justice, and the verse is the record of the command, the correction that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the full measure is the justice, and the one who commands is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who deals with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the command of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the justice: Shuayb commanded the full measure, and the believer who deals with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the justice that the measure requires.",
  "182": "“Weigh with an even balance,” continues the command, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse continues the command: or \"wa zinoo bil-qistasi al-mustaqeem,\" and the commentators note the continuation, for Shuayb commanded the balance, and the continuation is the correction, the demand of the justice, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the correction that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the balance is the justice, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the correction of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the continuation is the correction and the balance is the justice, and the verse is the record of the continuation, the correction that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the continuation, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the even balance is the justice, and the one who commands is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who deals with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the continuation of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the continuation is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the justice: Shuayb commanded the balance, and the believer who deals with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the continuation is the teaching that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the continuation, and the teaching of the justice that the balance requires.",
  "183": "“and do not defraud people of their property. Nor go about spreading corruption in the land.”” completes the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the command: or \"wa la tabkhasoo an-nasa ashya'ahum wa la ta'thaw fi al-ardi mufsideen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Shuayb forbade the defrauding and the corruption, and the completion is the correction, the two prohibitions, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the two prohibitions are the justice, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the correction and the two prohibitions are the justice, and the verse is the record of the completion, the correction that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own justice. The believer is taught that the defrauding and the corruption are the sins, and the one who forbids is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who deals with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the completion of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the command of the justice: Shuayb forbade the corruption, and the believer who deals with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the justice that the corruption requires.",
  "184": "“And fear the One Who created you and ˹all˺ earlier peoples.”” completes the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the command: or \"wattaqoo allathee khalaqakum wal-jibillata al-awwaleen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for Shuayb called the fear of the Creator, and the completion is the argument, the reminder of the creation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the creation is the argument, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the argument and the creation is the proof, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the fear of the Creator is the argument, and the one who declares is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who knows the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the evidence, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the creation: Shuayb declared the Creator, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "185": "“They replied, “You are simply bewitched!”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reply: or \"qaloo innama anta mina al-musahhareen,\" and the commentators note the reply, for the people accused the bewitchment, and the reply is the label, the charge of the sorcery, and the verse is the record of the reply, the label that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the reply, the label of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the label and the bewitchment is the charge, and the verse is the record of the reply, the label that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the label, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the magic is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Shuayb was labelled and answered, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "186": "“Also, you are only a human being like us, and we think you are indeed a liar.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: or \"wa ma anta illa basharun mithluna wa in nazunnuka la-mina al-kathibeen,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the people accused the lie, and the completion is the label, the charge of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the completion, the label that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the charge, and the verse is the record of the completion, the label of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the label and the lie is the charge, and the verse is the record of the completion, the label that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the lie is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the completion of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Shuayb was labelled and answered, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "187": "“So cause ˹deadly˺ pieces of the sky to fall upon us, if what you say is true.”” records the challenge, and the commentators note the two parts of the challenge. The verse records the challenge: or \"fa-asqit 'alayna kisafan mina as-sama'i in kunta mina as-sadiqeen,\" and the commentators note the challenge, for the people dared the sky, and the challenge is the dare, the demand of the punishment, and the verse is the record of the challenge, the dare that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the challenge is the dare, and the verse is the record of the challenge, the dare of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the challenge is the dare and the sky is the demand, and the verse is the record of the challenge, the dare that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the challenge, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own confidence. The believer is taught that the challenge of the falsehood is the dare, and the one who is dared is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the dare is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the challenge of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the dare is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the challenge is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the occasion of the challenge: Shuayb was dared and answered, and the believer who faces the dare is the believer whose truth the Lord displays, and the challenge is the teaching that the dare is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the challenge, and the teaching of the confidence that the challenge requires.",
  "188": "“Shu’aib responded, “My Lord knows best whatever you do.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: or \"qala rabbee a'lamu bima ta'maloon,\" and the commentators note the reply, for Shuayb attributed the knowledge to the Lord, and the reply is the reliance, the declaration of the trust, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reliance that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the attribution is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reliance of Shuayb. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the reliance and the attribution is the trust, and the verse is the record of the reply, the reliance that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the attribution of the knowledge is the reliance, and the one who trusts is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who leaves the judgment to the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord accepts, and the reply of Shuayb is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reliance of the prophet: Shuayb attributed the knowledge, and the believer who leaves the judgment to the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the trust that the reliance requires.",
  "189": "“So they rejected him, and ˹so˺ were overtaken by the torment of the day of the ˹deadly˺ cloud. That was really a torment of a tremendous day.”” records the punishment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the punishment: or \"fa-kaththaboohu fa-akhathahum 'athabu yawmi az-zullati innahu kana 'athaba yawmin 'azeem,\" and the commentators note the punishment, for the Lord overtook the people with the cloud, and the punishment is the judgment, the end of the Midian, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the cloud is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the punishment is the judgment and the cloud is the end, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the punishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own certainty. The believer is taught that the punishment of the rejection is the judgment, and the one who rejects is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the punishment of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rejection is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the rejection: the people rejected and were punished, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the punishment is the teaching that the rejection is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the punishment, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "190": "“Surely in this is a sign. Yet most of them would not believe.”” records the sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: or \"inna fee thalika la-ayatan wa ma kana aktharuhum mu'mineen,\" and the commentators note the sign, for the story is the sign, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the sign is the lesson, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sign is the lesson and the denial is the heedlessness, and the verse is the record of the sign, the reflection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the lessons, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the sign of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the sign: the story is the lesson, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the sign is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the reflection that the lesson invites.",
  "191": "“And your Lord is certainly the Almighty, Most Merciful.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: or \"wa inna rabbaka la-huwa al-'azeezu ar-raheem,\" and the commentators note the completion, for the two names close the story, and the completion is the praise, the declaration of the power and the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the chapter closes with, and the commentators note the significance, for the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the praise and the two names are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the completion, the praise that the chapter closes with.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer's own trust. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the trust, and the one who praises is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power and the mercy is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two names are the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa'di observes that the verse teaches the believer the two names: the Lord is the Almighty and the Merciful, and the believer who trusts the two is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the power and the mercy are the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the two names produce.",
  "192": "\"This is certainly a revelation from the Lord of all worlds,\" opens the chapter's word on the Book, and the verse is the teaching on the source, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the claim: \"This is certainly a revelation from the Lord of all worlds,\" and the word \"revelation\" is 'tanzil,' and the verse says the Quran is the sending down, and the commentators explain the meaning: the verse turns from the stories of the messengers to the source of the Book that tells them, and the turning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the Book is the revelation of the Lord of all worlds, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the spirit, and the spirit is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the source, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the source of the Book answers the accusation of the fabrication, and the answering is the chapter's word. The claim is the chapter's picture of the Book's standing, and the standing is the teaching, and the teaching is that the stories the chapter told are the revelation's, and the revelation's is the chapter's word, and the word opens the description of the descent, and the descent is the chapter's next teaching. The claim of the revelation opens the chapter's word on the Book, and the word teaches that the stories the chapter told are the revelation's, and the revelation's is the source's answer to the accusation.",
  "193": "\"which the trustworthy spirit ˹Gabriel˺ brought down\" continues the description, and the verse is the teaching on the spirit, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"which the trustworthy spirit ˹Gabriel˺ brought down\", and the word \"trustworthy spirit\" is 'ruh al-amin,' and the verse names the carrier, and the commentators explain the meaning: the trustworthy spirit is Gabriel, and the naming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the spirit, and the teaching is that the Book's carrier is the trusted angel, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the heart, and the heart is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the spirit, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the chain, and the teaching is that the revelation's chain is the Lord, the spirit, and the heart, and the chain is the chapter's word. The trustworthy spirit is the chapter's picture of the descent's purity, and the purity is the teaching, and the teaching is that the Book passed through the trusted to the trusted, and the passing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the description to its end, and the end is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the chain, and the chain is the chapter's teaching on the descent, and the teaching is that the Book passed through the trusted to the trusted, and the passing is the chapter's answer to the one who doubts the source, and the answer is that the chain of the Lord and the spirit and the heart is the pure, and the pure is the chapter's word.",
  "194": "\"into your heart ˹O Prophet˺—so that you may be one of the warners—\" continues the description, and the verse is the teaching on the heart, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"into your heart ˹O Prophet˺—so that you may be one of the warners—\" and the word \"heart\" is 'qalb,' and the verse names the descent's destination, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Book descended into the heart, and the descending is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the heart, and the teaching is that the revelation's place is the heart, and the verse then states the purpose: \"so that you may be one of the warners\", and the purpose is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mission, and the teaching is that the descent made the Prophet one of the warners, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the tongue, and the tongue is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the heart, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the revelation's home is the heart, and the home is the chapter's word, and the word carries the description to its closing, and the closing is the chapter's next teaching. The descent into the heart is the verse's stroke, and the stroke teaches that the revelation's home is the heart, for the heart is the place where the Book lives and works. The descent into the heart is the verse's stroke, and the stroke teaches that the revelation's home is the heart, for the heart is the place where the Book lives and works.",
  "195": "\"in a clear Arabic tongue\" completes the description, and the verse is the teaching on the tongue, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the description: \"in a clear Arabic tongue\", and the word \"clear\" is 'mubin,' and the verse names the language, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Book came in the clear Arabic, and the coming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the tongue, and the teaching is that the revelation's language is the people's clear tongue, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the foretelling, and the foretelling is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the tongue, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the clear Arabic is the Book's mercy to its first hearers, and the mercy is the chapter's word. The clear tongue is the chapter's picture of the Book's reach, and the reach is the teaching, and the teaching is that the revelation came in the language of its people, and the people's is the chapter's word, and the word carries the description to its seal, and the seal is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the mercy, and the mercy is the chapter's teaching on the tongue, and the teaching is that the clear Arabic is the Book's mercy to its first hearers, and the mercy is the chapter's answer to the one who asks why the Book came in the one tongue, and the answer is that the clear tongue is the reach, and the reach is the chapter's word.",
  "196": "\"And it has indeed been ˹foretold˺ in the Scriptures of those before\" completes the description, and the verse is the teaching on the foretelling, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the description: \"And it has indeed been ˹foretold˺ in the Scriptures of those before\", and the word \"foretold\" is 'zubur,' and the verse says the Book was mentioned in the earlier Scriptures, and the commentators explain the meaning: the earlier books carried the news of the final revelation, and the carrying is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the foretelling, and the teaching is that the Book is the chain's last link, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the scholars, and the scholars are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the foretelling, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the witness, and the teaching is that the earlier Scriptures are the Book's witnesses, and the witnesses are the chapter's word. The foretelling is the chapter's picture of the Book's continuity, and the continuity is the teaching, and the teaching is that the revelation is the one river through the ages, and the river is the chapter's word, and the word opens the chapter's next argument, and the argument is the chapter's next teaching. The foretelling in the earlier Scriptures is the verse's seal, and the seal teaches that the Book is the chain's last link, for the earlier books carried the news of the final revelation. The foretelling in the earlier Scriptures is the verse's seal, and the seal teaches that the Book is the chain's last link, for the earlier books carried the news of the final revelation.",
  "197": "\"Was it not sufficient proof for the deniers that it has been recognized by the knowledgeable among the Children of Israel?\" states the chapter's witness, and the verse is the teaching on the recognition, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Was it not sufficient proof for the deniers that it has been recognized by the knowledgeable among the Children of Israel?\" and the word \"recognized\" is 'ya'lamuhu,' and the verse names the witness, and the commentators explain the meaning: the scholars of the Children of Israel knew the Book's foretelling, and the knowing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the recognition, and the teaching is that the scholars' knowledge is the proof of the Book, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the non-Arab, and the non-Arab is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the recognition, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the witness, and the teaching is that the earlier Scriptures' scholars are the Book's witnesses, and the witnesses are the chapter's word. The question is the chapter's picture of the proof's sufficiency, and the sufficiency is the teaching, and the teaching is that the deniers' own neighbours knew the truth, and the knowing is the chapter's word, and the word opens the chapter's next argument, and the argument is the chapter's next teaching. The question of the sufficiency is the chapter's witness, and the witness teaches that the scholars' knowledge is the proof, for the deniers' own neighbours knew the Book's foretelling. The question of the sufficiency is the chapter's witness, and the witness teaches that the scholars' knowledge is the proof, for the deniers' own neighbours knew the Book's foretelling.",
  "198": "\"Had We revealed it to a non-Arab,\" opens the chapter's argument, and the verse is the teaching on the objection, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the imagined: \"Had We revealed it to a non-Arab,\" and the word \"non-Arab\" is 'a'jami,' and the verse imagines the alternative, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers had objected that the Book's carrier was an Arab, and the verse answers the objection by the imagined, and the answering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the objection, and the teaching is that the objection's imagined answer is the objection's end, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the recitation, and the recitation is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the objection, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the imagined alternative is the proof that the objection was the excuse, and the excuse is the chapter's word. The imagined non-Arab is the chapter's picture of the argument's method, and the method is the teaching, and the teaching is that the verse answers the objection by removing the objection's ground, and the removing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its end, and the end is the chapter's next teaching. The imagined non-Arab is the verse's method, and the method teaches that the answer to the objection is the removal of the objection's ground, for the imagined alternative is the objection's end. The imagined non-Arab is the verse's method, and the method teaches that the answer to the objection is the removal of the ground, for the imagined alternative is the objection's end.",
  "199": "\"who would then recite it to the deniers ˹in fluent Arabic˺, still they would not have believed in it!\" completes the argument, and the verse is the teaching on the refusal, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the argument: \"who would then recite it to the deniers ˹in fluent Arabic˺, still they would not have believed in it!\" and the verse completes the imagined, and the commentators explain the meaning: even the non-Arab reciting the fluent Arabic would not turn them, and the not turning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the refusal, and the teaching is that the denial is not the language's but the heart's, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the steeping, and the steeping is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the refusal, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the root, and the teaching is that the refused is not the reciter but the recited, and the recited is the chapter's word. The imagined recitation is the chapter's picture of the argument's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the denial's ground is the heart's, and the heart's is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's next teaching. The refusal of the imagined recitation is the verse's verdict, and the verdict teaches that the denial is not the language's but the heart's, for the refused is not the reciter but the recited. The refusal of the imagined recitation is the verse's verdict, and the verdict teaches that the denial is not the language's but the heart's, for the refused is not the reciter but the recited.",
  "200": "\"This is how We allow denial ˹to steep˺ into the hearts of the wicked\" states the verdict, and the verse is the teaching on the steeping, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the verdict: \"This is how We allow denial ˹to steep˺ into the hearts of the wicked\", and the word \"steep\" is 'salaknahu,' and the verse says the denial is made to enter the wicked hearts, and the commentators explain the meaning: the wicked heart's own refusal becomes its fixed state, and the fixing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the steeping, and the teaching is that the denial is the heart's own earning, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the punishment, and the punishment is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the steeping, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the wicked heart receives its own refusal, and the receiving is the chapter's word. The steeping is the chapter's picture of the denial's depth, and the depth is the teaching, and the teaching is that the refused truth becomes the fixed falsehood, and the fixing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the verdict to its consequence, and the consequence is the chapter's next teaching. The steeping of the denial is the verse's justice, and the justice teaches that the wicked heart receives its own refusal, for the refused truth becomes the fixed falsehood. The steeping of the denial is the verse's justice, and the justice teaches that the wicked heart receives its own refusal, for the refused truth becomes the fixed falsehood.",
  "201": "\"They will not believe in it until they see the painful punishment,\" states the consequence, and the verse is the teaching on the too-late, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the consequence: \"They will not believe in it until they see the painful punishment,\" and the word \"until\" is the verse's limit, and the verse says the belief comes at the punishment, and the commentators explain the meaning: the belief at the punishment is not the belief, and the not-belief is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the too-late, and the teaching is that the seeing of the punishment closes the door of the belief, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the surprise, and the surprise is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the too-late, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the door, and the teaching is that the world was the door, and the door is the chapter's word. The until is the chapter's picture of the denial's end, and the end is the teaching, and the teaching is that the belief at the punishment is the confession of the caught, and the confession is the chapter's word, and the word carries the consequence to its scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching. The until of the punishment is the verse's limit, and the limit teaches that the belief at the punishment is the confession of the caught, for the world was the door. The until of the punishment is the verse's limit, and the limit teaches that the belief at the punishment is the confession of the caught, for the world was the door.",
  "202": "\"which will take them by surprise when they least expect ˹it˺\" continues the consequence, and the verse is the teaching on the surprise, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"which will take them by surprise when they least expect ˹it˺\", and the word \"surprise\" is 'baghtatan,' and the verse says the punishment comes at the unexpecting, and the commentators explain the meaning: the punishment's suddenness is its first stroke, and the suddenness is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the surprise, and the teaching is that the denied punishment comes at the denied hour, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the cry, and the cry is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the surprise, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the hour, and the teaching is that the unexpected hour is the punishment's shape, and the shape is the chapter's word. The surprise is the chapter's picture of the end's manner, and the manner is the teaching, and the teaching is that the one who put off the belief meets the unpostponed, and the meeting is the chapter's word, and the word carries the scene to its cry, and the cry is the chapter's next teaching. The surprise of the punishment is the verse's stroke, and the stroke teaches that the denied punishment comes at the denied hour, for the unexpected is the punishment's shape. The surprise of the punishment is the verse's stroke, and the stroke teaches that the denied punishment comes at the denied hour, for the unexpected is the punishment's shape.",
  "203": "\"Then they will cry,\"\"Can we be allowed more time?\" completes the scene, and the verse is the teaching on the cry, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the scene: \"Then they will cry, \"Can we be allowed more time?\"\" and the word \"time\" is 'munzarun,' and the verse records the cry of the postponement, and the commentators explain the meaning: the asked postponement is the denial's last hope, and the hope is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the cry, and the teaching is that the asked time is the closed door, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the hastening, and the hastening is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the cry, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the world was the time, and the time is the chapter's word. The cry is the chapter's picture of the regret's depth, and the depth is the teaching, and the teaching is that the one who asked the hastening asks the postponing, and the asking is the chapter's word, and the word carries the scene to its answer, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the answer, and the answer is the time, and the time is the chapter's teaching on the door, and the teaching is that the world was the time, and the time is the chapter's answer to the one who postpones, and the answer is that the asked postponement is the closed door, and the door is the chapter's word.",
  "204": "\"Do they ˹really˺ seek to hasten Our torment?\" asks the answer, and the verse is the teaching on the hastening, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Do they ˹really˺ seek to hasten Our torment?\" and the word \"hasten\" is 'yasta'jilun,' and the verse records the irony, and the commentators explain the meaning: the same deniers who will cry for the time now ask for the hastening, and the asking is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the hastening, and the teaching is that the dared punishment is the begged postponement, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the years, and the years are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the hastening, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the irony, and the teaching is that the mocker's dare is the caught's cry, and the two are the one tongue, and the one is the chapter's word. The question is the chapter's picture of the denial's two faces, and the faces are the teaching, and the teaching is that the hastening of the mockery and the begging of the end are the same heart's two words, and the words are the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its next scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching. The question of the hastening is the verse's irony, and the irony teaches that the mocker's dare is the caught's cry, for the hastening and the begging are the one tongue. The question of the hastening is the verse's irony, and the irony teaches that the mocker's dare is the caught's cry, for the hastening and the begging are the one tongue.",
  "205": "\"Imagine ˹O Prophet˺ if We allowed them enjoyment for years,\" opens the chapter's imagined, and the verse is the teaching on the years, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the imagined: \"Imagine ˹O Prophet˺ if We allowed them enjoyment for years,\" and the word \"years\" is 'sinin,' and the verse imagines the long enjoyment, and the commentators explain the meaning: the imagined years are the answer to the hastening, and the answering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the years, and the teaching is that the long enjoyment is the test's length, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the coming, and the coming is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the years, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the measure, and the teaching is that the years of the enjoyment are the nothing before the threat, and the nothing is the chapter's word. The imagined is the chapter's picture of the argument's method, and the method is the teaching, and the teaching is that the verse takes the denier's own wish and shows its end, and the showing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its close, and the close is the chapter's next teaching. The imagined years are the verse's method, and the method teaches that the long enjoyment is the test's length, for the verse takes the denier's own wish and shows its end. The imagined years are the verse's method, and the method teaches that the long enjoyment is the test's length, for the verse takes the denier's own wish and shows its end.",
  "206": "\"then there came to them what they had been threatened with:\" continues the imagined, and the verse is the teaching on the coming, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"then there came to them what they had been threatened with:\" and the verse completes the imagined, and the commentators explain the meaning: the threat's coming is the years' end, and the ending is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the coming, and the teaching is that the warned threat is the certain, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the benefit, and the benefit is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the coming, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is that the delayed is not the cancelled, and the cancelled of nothing is the chapter's word. The coming is the chapter's picture of the argument's hinge, and the hinge is the teaching, and the teaching is that the years and the threat are the one story's two halves, and the halves are the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its question, and the question is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the certainty, and the certainty is the chapter's teaching on the coming, and the teaching is that the delayed is not the cancelled, and the cancelled of nothing is the chapter's answer to the one who reads the delay as the safety, and the answer is that the warned threat is the certain, and the certain is the chapter's word. The coming of the threat is the verse's hinge, and the hinge teaches that the delayed is not the cancelled, for the warned threat is the certain.",
  "207": "\"would that enjoyment be of any benefit to them ˹at all˺?\" closes the imagined, and the verse is the teaching on the benefit, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse closes the imagined: \"would that enjoyment be of any benefit to them ˹at all˺?\" and the word \"benefit\" is 'yughni,' and the verse asks the question, and the commentators explain the meaning: the years of the enjoyment are the nothing when the threat comes, and the nothing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the benefit, and the teaching is that the enjoyed years do not buy the one hour, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the warners, and the warners are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the benefit, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the measure, and the teaching is that the world's whole enjoyment is the nothing before the threat, and the nothing is the chapter's word. The question is the chapter's picture of the argument's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the one who measured by the years is measured by the hour, and the measuring is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its law, and the law is the chapter's next teaching. The question of the benefit is the verse's close, and the close teaches that the world's whole enjoyment is the nothing before the threat, for the one who measured by the years is measured by the hour. The question of the benefit is the verse's close, and the close teaches that the world's whole enjoyment is the nothing before the threat, for the years are measured by the hour.",
  "208": "\"We have never destroyed a society without warners\" states the law, and the verse is the teaching on the warners, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the law: \"We have never destroyed a society without warners\", and the word \"warners\" is 'mundhirin,' and the verse says the destruction is never without the warning, and the commentators explain the meaning: the warning precedes the destruction, and the preceding is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the warners, and the teaching is that the justice of God sends the warner before the punishment, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reminding, and the reminding is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the warners, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the warned are the punished, and the unwarned are the spared, and the sparing is the chapter's word. The law is the chapter's picture of the justice's order, and the order is the teaching, and the teaching is that the warning is the mercy's first word, and the word is the chapter's word, and the word carries the law to its ground, and the ground is the chapter's next teaching. The law of the warners is the verse's justice, and the justice teaches that the warning precedes the destruction, for the warned are the punished and the unwarned are the spared. The law of the warners is the verse's justice, and the justice teaches that the warning precedes the destruction, for the warned are the punished and the unwarned are the spared.",
  "209": "\"to remind ˹them˺, for We would never wrong ˹anyone˺\" completes the law, and the verse is the teaching on the reminding, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the law: \"to remind ˹them˺, for We would never wrong ˹anyone˺\", and the word \"remind\" is 'dhikra,' and the verse names the warning's purpose, and the commentators explain the meaning: the reminding is the mercy, and the never-wronging is the justice, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the reminding, and the teaching is that the reminder precedes the account, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the devils, and the devils are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the reminding, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the never-wronging is the law's seal, and the seal is the chapter's word. The reminding is the chapter's picture of the mercy's order, and the order is the teaching, and the teaching is that the reminder and the justice are the one God's two hands, and the hands are the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its next argument, and the argument is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the order, and the order is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the reminder and the justice are the one God's two hands, and the hands are the chapter's answer to the one who asks about the law's ground, and the answer is that the never-wronging is the law's seal, and the seal is the chapter's word.",
  "210": "\"It was not the devils who brought this ˹Quran˺ down:\" opens the chapter's defense, and the verse is the teaching on the devils, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the defense: \"It was not the devils who brought this ˹Quran˺ down:\" and the word \"devils\" is 'shayatin,' and the verse denies the devils' descent, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers had claimed the Book's source was the devils, and the claim is the verse's target, and the denial is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the devils, and the teaching is that the Book's source is not the denied, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the fitting, and the fitting is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the devils, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the Book that calls to the good is not the devils' work, and the working of nothing is the chapter's word. The defense is the chapter's picture of the argument's opening, and the opening is the teaching, and the teaching is that the verse clears the Book of the false source, and the clearing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the defense to its second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's next teaching. The denial of the devils is the verse's defense, and the defense teaches that the Book's source is not the denied, for the Book that calls to the good is not the devils' work.",
  "211": "\"it is not for them ˹to do so˺, nor can they,\" continues the defense, and the verse is the teaching on the fitting, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"it is not for them ˹to do so˺, nor can they,\" and the verse records the two negations, and the commentators explain the meaning: the devils neither deserve the descent nor have the power of it, and the two negations are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the fitting, and the teaching is that the Book's descent is the devils' impossible, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the barring, and the barring is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the fitting, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the Book's carrier is the trustworthy, and the devils are the barred, and the barred is the chapter's word. The two negations are the chapter's picture of the defense's second stroke, and the stroke is the teaching, and the teaching is that the deserved and the capable are the two tests of the source, and the tests are the chapter's word, and the word carries the defense to its seal, and the seal is the chapter's next teaching. The two negations are the defense's second stroke, and the stroke teaches that the deserved and the capable are the two tests of the source, and the devils fail both. The two negations are the defense's second stroke, and the stroke teaches that the deserved and the capable are the two tests of the source, and the devils fail both tests.",
  "212": "\"for they are strictly barred from ˹even˺ overhearing ˹it˺\" completes the defense, and the verse is the teaching on the barring, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the defense: \"for they are strictly barred from ˹even˺ overhearing ˹it˺\", and the word \"barred\" is 'ma'zulun,' and the verse records the barring, and the commentators explain the meaning: the devils are cut off from the heavenly hearing, and the cutting is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the barring, and the teaching is that the devils cannot even overhear the revelation, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the command, and the command is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the barring, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the one who cannot overhear cannot deliver, and the delivering of nothing is the chapter's word. The barring is the chapter's picture of the defense's seal, and the seal is the teaching, and the teaching is that the Book's source is the pure, and the pure is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its command, and the command is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the seal, and the seal is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the one who cannot overhear cannot deliver, and the delivering of nothing is the chapter's answer to the one who claims the devils' source, and the answer is that the Book's source is the pure, and the pure is the chapter's word.",
  "213": "\"So do not ever call upon any other god besides Allah, or you will be one of the punished\" states the command, and the verse is the teaching on the warning, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the command: \"So do not ever call upon any other god besides Allah, or you will be one of the punished\", and the word \"call\" is 'tad'u,' and the verse commands the purity, and the commentators explain the meaning: the command to the Prophet is the command to every caller, and the warning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the warning, and the teaching is that the calling on the other is the punished, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the clan, and the clan is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the warning, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the purity, and the teaching is that the one God is the called, and the other is the never, and the never is the chapter's word. The command is the chapter's picture of the defense's fruit, and the fruit is the teaching, and the teaching is that the Book's defense ends in the worship's purity, and the purity is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its great command, and the command is the chapter's next teaching. The command of the purity is the defense's fruit, and the fruit teaches that the Book's defense ends in the worship's purity, for the one God is the called and the other is the never.",
  "214": "\"And warn ˹all, starting with˺ your closest relatives,\" states the great command, and the verse is the teaching on the clan, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the command: \"And warn ˹all, starting with˺ your closest relatives,\" and the word \"closest\" is 'ashirataka,' and the verse commands the warning of the clan first, and the commentators explain the meaning: the warning begins at the nearest, and the beginning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the clan, and the teaching is that the messenger's first duty is his own household, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the graciousness, and the graciousness is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the clan, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the order, and the teaching is that the nearest first is the mercy's order, and the order is the chapter's word. The command is the chapter's picture of the messenger's first step, and the step is the teaching, and the teaching is that the warning of the clan is the warning of the world's beginning, and the beginning is the chapter's word, and the word carries the command to its second half, and the half is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the order, and the order is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the nearest first is the mercy's order, and the order is the chapter's answer to the one who asks where the warning begins, and the answer is that the warning of the clan is the warning of the world's beginning, and the beginning is the chapter's word.",
  "215": "\"and be gracious to the believers who follow you\" continues the command, and the verse is the teaching on the graciousness, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"and be gracious to the believers who follow you\", and the word \"gracious\" is 'khfid janahaka,' the word of the lowering of the wing, and the verse records the command of the tenderness, and the commentators explain the meaning: the messenger's wing is lowered over the followers, and the lowering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the graciousness, and the teaching is that the tenderness is the messenger's manner, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the disobeying, and the disobeying is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the graciousness, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the lowered wing is the leadership's sign, and the sign is the chapter's word. The command is the chapter's picture of the messenger's two manners, and the manners are the teaching, and the teaching is that the warning of the clan and the tenderness to the followers are the one mission's two faces, and the faces are the chapter's word, and the word carries the command to its closing, and the closing is the chapter's next teaching. The command of the lowered wing is the verse's tenderness, and the tenderness teaches that the messenger's wing is lowered over the followers, for the leadership of the faith is the service. The command of the lowered wing is the verse's tenderness, and the tenderness teaches that the messenger's wing is lowered over the followers, for the leadership of the faith is the service.",
  "216": "\"But if they disobey you, say,\"\"I am certainly free of what you do\" closes the command, and the verse is the teaching on the freedom, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse closes the command: \"But if they disobey you, say, \"I am certainly free of what you do,\"\" and the word \"free\" is 'bari',' and the verse records the declaration, and the commentators explain the meaning: the messenger's duty ends at the delivery, and the disowning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the freedom, and the teaching is that the deliverer is free of the refuser's deed, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the trust, and the trust is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the freedom, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the boundary, and the teaching is that the warning and the disowning are the messenger's two ends, and the ends are the chapter's word. The declaration is the chapter's picture of the mission's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the delivered warning and the declared freedom are the messenger's complete duty, and the duty is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its closing, and the closing is the chapter's next teaching. The declaration of the freedom is the mission's close, and the close teaches that the delivered warning and the declared freedom are the messenger's complete duty, and the duty ends at the delivery. The declaration of the freedom is the mission's close, and the close teaches that the delivered warning and the declared freedom are the messenger's complete duty, and the duty ends at the delivery.",
  "217": "\"Put your trust in the Almighty, Most Merciful\" opens the chapter's closing commands, and the verse is the teaching on the trust, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the command: \"Put your trust in the Almighty, Most Merciful,\" and the word \"trust\" is 'tawakkal,' and the verse commands the reliance on the two names, and the commentators explain the meaning: the closing commands of the chapter begin with the trust, and the beginning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the trust, and the teaching is that the messenger's reliance is on the Almighty and the Merciful, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the seeing, and the seeing is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the trust, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the two names, and the teaching is that the chapter's every story ended at the two names, and now the two names are the trust's place, and the placing is the chapter's word. The trust is the chapter's picture of the believer's posture, and the posture is the teaching, and the teaching is that the one who relies on the Almighty and the Merciful is the one who is seen and heard, and the seeing is the chapter's next verses. The command of the trust opens the chapter's close, and the opening teaches that the believer's reliance is on the two names, for the Almighty and the Merciful are the chapter's standing pair. The command of the trust opens the chapter's close, and the opening teaches that the believer's reliance is on the two names, for the Almighty and the Merciful are the chapter's pair.",
  "218": "\"Who sees you when you rise ˹for prayer at night˺,\" continues the command, and the verse is the teaching on the seeing, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"Who sees you when you rise ˹for prayer at night˺,\" and the word \"sees\" is 'yaraka,' and the verse records the seeing of the night prayer, and the commentators explain the meaning: the trust is grounded in the seeing, and the grounding is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the seeing, and the teaching is that the messenger's night rising is seen by his Lord, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the movements, and the movements are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the seeing, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the comfort, and the teaching is that the one whose rising is seen is not alone, and the aloneness of nothing is the chapter's word. The seeing is the chapter's picture of the trust's ground, and the ground is the teaching, and the teaching is that the night prayer is the trust's proof, and the proof is the chapter's word, and the word carries the command to its second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's next teaching. The seeing of the rising is the trust's ground, and the ground teaches that the one whose night rising is seen is not alone, for the night prayer is the trust's proof. The seeing of the rising is the trust's ground, and the ground teaches that the one whose night rising is seen is not alone, for the night prayer is the trust's proof.",
  "219": "\"as well as your movements ˹in prayer˺ along with ˹fellow˺ worshippers\" completes the command, and the verse is the teaching on the movements, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the command: \"as well as your movements ˹in prayer˺ along with ˹fellow˺ worshippers\", and the word \"movements\" is 'taqallubaka,' and the verse records the seeing of the prayer's movements, and the commentators explain the meaning: the bowing and the rising among the worshippers are seen, and the seeing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the movements, and the teaching is that the prayer's every movement is in the Lord's sight, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the hearing, and the hearing is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the movements, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the company, and the teaching is that the worshipper's movements with the fellow worshippers are the seen, and the seen is the chapter's word. The movements are the chapter's picture of the prayer's fullness, and the fullness is the teaching, and the teaching is that the trust of the messenger is the trust of the one whose every bow is seen, and the seeing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the command to its seal, and the seal is the chapter's next teaching. The movements among the worshippers are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke teaches that the prayer's every bow is in the Lord's sight, for the seen and the heard are the one knowing. The movements among the worshippers are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke teaches that the prayer's every bow is in the Lord's sight, for the seen and the heard are the one knowing.",
  "220": "\"He ˹alone˺ is indeed the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\" seals the command, and the verse is the teaching on the two names, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse seals the command: \"He ˹alone˺ is indeed the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\", and the word \"All-Hearing\" is 'sami',' and the word \"All-Knowing\" is 'alim,' and the verse pairs the two, and the commentators explain the meaning: the hearing of the prayer's words and the knowing of the heart's state are the trust's ground, and the pairing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the two names, and the teaching is that the seen and the heard are the one Lord's two knowings, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the devils, and the devils are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the two names, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the trust's seal, and the teaching is that the one who trusts the Hearing and the Knowing has trusted the enough, and the enough is the chapter's word. The two names are the chapter's picture of the trust's full ground, and the ground is the teaching, and the teaching is that the trust of the believer is the trust of the seen and the heard, and the two are the chapter's word, and the word opens the chapter's closing argument, and the argument is the chapter's next teaching. The two names of the hearing and the knowing seal the command, and the seal teaches that the trust of the believer is the trust of the seen and the heard, and the two are the enough.",
  "221": "\"Shall I inform you of whom the devils ˹actually˺ descend upon?\" opens the closing argument, and the verse is the teaching on the descent, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse asks the question: \"Shall I inform you of whom the devils ˹actually˺ descend upon?\" and the word \"descend\" is 'tanazzalu,' and the verse turns the claim against the claimants, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers had claimed the Prophet's source was the devils, and the verse asks about the devils' true receivers, and the asking is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the descent, and the teaching is that the question turns the accusation back, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the liar, and the liar is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the descent, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the method, and the teaching is that the answer to the accusation is the exposure of the accuser, and the exposure is the chapter's word. The question is the chapter's picture of the argument's opening, and the opening is the teaching, and the teaching is that the devils' descent is the known, and the known is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its answer, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The question of the descent is the argument's opening, and the opening teaches that the answer to the accusation is the exposure of the accuser, for the question turns the claim back. The question of the descent is the argument's opening, and the opening teaches that the answer to the accusation is the exposure of the accuser, for the question turns the claim back.",
  "222": "\"They descend upon every sinful liar,\" answers the question, and the verse is the teaching on the liar, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse answers the question: \"They descend upon every sinful liar,\" and the word \"sinful liar\" is 'affakin athim,' and the verse names the devils' receivers, and the commentators explain the meaning: the devils descend on the lying and the sinning, and the descending is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the liar, and the teaching is that the devils' company is the liar's, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the ear, and the ear is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the liar, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the contrast, and the teaching is that the truthful messenger and the lying soothsayer are the two receivers, and the two are the chapter's word. The naming is the chapter's picture of the argument's point, and the point is the teaching, and the teaching is that the devils' descent is the falsehood's, and the Book's descent is the truth's, and the two descents are the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its detail, and the detail is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's teaching on the two receivers, and the teaching is that the truthful messenger and the lying soothsayer are the two, and the two are the chapter's answer to the accusation, and the answer is that the devils' descent is the falsehood's, and the falsehood's is the chapter's word.",
  "223": "\"who gives an ˹attentive˺ ear ˹to half-truths˺, mostly passing on sheer lies\" completes the argument, and the verse is the teaching on the ear, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the argument: \"who gives an ˹attentive˺ ear ˹to half-truths˺, mostly passing on sheer lies\", and the word \"ear\" is 'yulqun as-sam',' and the verse records the soothsayer's manner, and the commentators explain the meaning: the devils' receiver hears the scraps and passes the lies, and the passing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the ear, and the teaching is that the false receiver's stock is the mixture of the scrap and the lie, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the poets, and the poets are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the ear, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the difference, and the teaching is that the Prophet's source is the clear and the soothsayer's is the mixed, and the two are the chapter's word. The detail is the chapter's picture of the falsehood's stock, and the stock is the teaching, and the teaching is that the false receiver's word is the mostly-lie, and the mostly is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its last argument, and the argument is the chapter's next teaching. The mixture of the scrap and the lie is the verse's detail, and the detail teaches that the false receiver's stock is the mostly-lie, for the devils' receiver hears the scraps and passes the lies. The mixture of the scrap and the lie is the verse's detail, and the detail teaches that the false receiver's stock is the mostly-lie, for the devils' receiver hears the scraps and passes the lies.",
  "224": "\"As for poets, they are followed ˹merely˺ by deviants\" opens the last argument, and the verse is the teaching on the poets, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the argument: \"As for poets, they are followed ˹merely˺ by deviants\", and the word \"poets\" is 'shu'ara',' and the word \"deviants\" is 'ghawun,' and the verse records the claim, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers had called the Prophet a poet, and the verse answers the claim by describing the poets' followers, and the describing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the poets, and the teaching is that the poet's follower is the strayer, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the ranting, and the ranting is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the poets, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the answer, and the teaching is that the claim of the poetry is answered by the difference of the two, and the difference is the chapter's word. The argument is the chapter's picture of the defense's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the Book is not the poetry, and the poetry of nothing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its detail, and the detail is the chapter's next teaching. The argument of the poets is the defense's close, and the close teaches that the poet's follower is the strayer, for the word of the mood is not the word of the truth.",
  "225": "\"Do you not see how they rant in every field,\" continues the argument, and the verse is the teaching on the ranting, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"Do you not see how they rant in every field,\" and the word \"rant\" is 'ya'imun,' and the verse records the poets' wandering, and the commentators explain the meaning: the poet wanders through every valley of the word, and the wandering is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the ranting, and the teaching is that the poet's word follows the mood, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the saying, and the saying is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the ranting, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the difference, and the teaching is that the poet's wandering and the Book's straightness are the two, and the two are the chapter's word. The ranting is the chapter's picture of the poetry's nature, and the nature is the teaching, and the teaching is that the word of the mood is not the word of the truth, and the truth is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its close, and the close is the chapter's next teaching. The wandering of the poets is the verse's stroke, and the stroke teaches that the poet wanders through every valley of the word, for the word of the mood follows the mood. The wandering of the poets is the verse's stroke, and the stroke teaches that the poet wanders through every valley of the word, for the word of the mood follows the mood.",
  "226": "\"only saying what they never do?\" completes the argument, and the verse is the teaching on the saying, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the argument: \"only saying what they never do?\" and the word \"saying\" is 'yaqulun,' and the verse records the poets' gap, and the commentators explain the meaning: the poet's word and the poet's deed are the two, and the gap is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the saying, and the teaching is that the one whose word outruns the deed is not the messenger, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the exception, and the exception is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the saying, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the difference, and the teaching is that the Prophet's word and deed are the one, and the one is the chapter's word. The gap is the chapter's picture of the poetry's mark, and the mark is the teaching, and the teaching is that the Book's carrier is not the sayer of the undone, and the undone is the chapter's word, and the word carries the argument to its exception, and the exception is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the difference, and the difference is the chapter's teaching on the one, and the teaching is that the Prophet's word and deed are the one, and the one is the chapter's answer to the claim of the poetry, and the answer is that the Book's carrier is not the sayer of the undone, and the undone is the chapter's word.",
  "227": "\"Except those who believe, do good, remember Allah often, and ˹poetically˺ avenge ˹the believers˺ after being wrongfully slandered. The wrongdoers will come to know what ˹evil˺ end they will meet\" closes the chapter, and the verse is the teaching on the exception, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the exception: \"Except those who believe, do good, remember Allah often, and ˹poetically˺ avenge ˹the believers˺ after being wrongfully slandered\", and the word \"except\" is 'illa,' and the verse names the believing poets, and the commentators explain the meaning: the poet who believes and does the good and remembers and answers the slander with the truth is the excepted, and the excepting is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the exception, and the teaching is that the word in the service of the truth is not the poetry's wandering, and the verse then states the warning: \"The wrongdoers will come to know what ˹evil˺ end they will meet\", and the warning is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the chapter closes with the warning to the wrongdoers, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the chapter's closing, and the closing is the chapter's word. The verse is the chapter's word on the exception, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the truthful word is the excepted, and the false word is the warned, and the two are the chapter's word, and the word closes the chapter of the poets, and the closing is the chapter's final teaching. The exception of the believing poets is the chapter's balance, and the balance teaches that the word in the service of the truth is the excepted, and the warning to the wrongdoers is the chapter's close."
};
